[{"id": "740f3883-8d34-4ebb-8a81-dcdc86f4f7cf", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:32:00): I love that hybrid approach. That's a really cool case study.\n\nLiv Faris (00:32:03): Yeah, we'll see how it goes. We're in the thick of it.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:32:08): So to use this Portugal example on a project like that, why bring an outside production company versus keeping that all in house? I think the more maybe concrete examples we can give to folks about distinguishing that, why I think it's going to be really helpful.\n\nLiv Faris (00:32:28): Yeah, I mean on that one right now we just don't have the capabilities in-house to do that. Right.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:32:35): And when you say capabilities, can you", "token_estimate": 161, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:32:00", "end_timestamp": "00:32:35", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "1732d795-493c-47e2-b0a5-f54c7c0c3dcc", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Liv Faris (00:32:37): Elaborate on We don't have anybody that video. We don't have editors. I have producers that can line produce, but I don't, I mean, I have a list of crew that I could crew up if I needed to, but in this case, we knew that we wanted to shoot in Europe or South America. We also considered. So there's the complexity of that. The schedule on this project is not super tight, but not as long as ideally story of our lives. There's a lot of deliverables, bowls, so I think it's a more, right now, these more complex projects, it's definitely helpful to have a production company. And the one that we've worked with on this project has been amazing, super organized project managing really well, found an amazing local fixer in Portugal. It just has experience shooting the type of content that we were shooting. So I think there was just a lot of benefits to that. (00:33:50): And then also this was a project that we had so much going on and I took on this project because all my other producers were busy, and so my capacity to line produce something like this just isn't there. So I just think it was a range of reasons. And so it's that complexity, getting the experience, the contacts, those kinds of things, time constraints on my side. We still tried to take as much as we could. We did the creative development, we did the storyboarding, we did the copywriting, all of that. So there was still a lot of things that we did on our internal team, but the production piece of it went to the production company. So I think that's worth mentioning is that there are projects where we also need creative development support because our designers and copywriters and creative directors have a lot on their plates too. (00:35:01): So sometimes we need that support. But that's one area where I think we've been able to do more of that in-house is writing the script. So you ask for examples. So for example, we did a brand campaign recently, and that was really the moment where I was talking with Mike, who's our VP of creative about this earlier this week, just about that was a moment where it just became very clear that our in-house team understood our brand, were much better suited to develop the creative for this campaign than an outside partner. And so that was a big moment for us of bringing that in-house. And so we wrote all the scripts, we did all the creative development, but we still needed the production partner to pull it off. We worked with a vendor on that, we hired a director, all of that. Maybe that's a good example to show where I think we're getting more and more comfortable with the creative development and now we just need to get the production side of it. From my perspective, we need to get the team in place to be able to do more of that in-house too.", "token_estimate": 701, "speakers": ["Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:32:37", "end_timestamp": "00:32:37", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "0d279cc3-2b08-43b2-816b-8b96a8ac9fbf", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:36:31): So I feel like you brought up another great example as to why the debate between in-house versus out an external vendor. A lot of people bring up the idea of the outside consultant perspective where a lot of the reason agencies get hired is because they have a different perspective. They come in with fresh ideas, they see things from a different vantage point for your team. I guess what does it take for an internal team to be able to still deliver that type of creative? And I dunno if I necessarily believe the outside perspective is always a better perspective vantage point. I think it's very project dependent, but for your team, what do you think allowed you guys to feel confident knowing that the creative that you had found that you had locked down was a good direction?", "token_estimate": 202, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:36:31", "end_timestamp": "00:36:31", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "d1e9c0f4-fa35-484e-a077-36a13b0b425c", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Liv Faris (00:37:23): Yeah, I think on that particular project, sharing scripts and sharing creative with our partners outside of the studio, our marketing partners, our CEO of what resonates. I think it was very clear that the work that we had done in-house was resonating and felt more in line with our brand than an outside perspective did in that case. And I think it's easy to get kind of stuck or to not have the time to refresh yourself and keep that creative energy going. And so there can be times I think where it's helpful to have an outside agency or vendor or somebody come in to help with that, but ultimately they're not going to know the brand as well as we know the brand. And we've seen that in a project that we did earlier this year. We had a vendor that was helping us write scripts, and they sent us scripts and we rewrote them because it just didn't feel in line with our brand. (00:38:39): But I don't want to discount the value of outside partners because there's always going to be a need for that. I think it's just we have a lot of talent here and people that are passionate for the sport of running passionate for the story and the values that Brooks is telling, and we're living it every day. You don't have to be a runner to work at Brooks, but most of us are. And so when we're talking about trail running, for example, the team that's working on our trail running, they are trail runners themselves. They're out there doing it, they know what's happening in the industry. They know what do people wear when they go trail running, things like that versus an outside partner that may need to get themselves up to speed on that. Right. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:39:35): If you could think about all the deliverables that you have created over the last year, how many percentage wise, how many of those have been in-house, how many those have been outside?", "token_estimate": 471, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:37:23", "end_timestamp": "00:39:35", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "7b38be58-75a4-4248-b00b-f48414fd2da2", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Liv Faris (00:39:48): I mean just from a pure production standpoint or including creative development? I mean, I would say it's more heavily weighted to outside. Again, just because from a production standpoint, we just don't have the resources, but I've seen it. It's shifting. It's shifting. And as we go into this next round of work, which I've got a long list for what's next, I'm really looking at that of, okay, how do we do this differently? I think the biggest thing that we've seen on my team is the photography side, having that in-house photographer that's able to just take those requests and go and do them. So that's definitely been a shift. Those were hiring an outside photographer and now our PR team, our Brooks Run club team, Gavin's shooting a lot of that. A lot of our product stuff for our special products around 4th of July or St. Patty's Day or whatever it may be. We're shooting more of that. So I don't know the exact percentage split, but definitely it's changing. It's changing as we go. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:41:02): Do you see other companies in the industry making similar decisions? Do you see them bringing more and more capabilities?", "token_estimate": 293, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:39:48", "end_timestamp": "00:41:02", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "293bb3e8-3fab-4ad4-a909-44d0c1e8b2da", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Liv Faris (00:41:08): Yeah, so one of the things that I did earlier this year was as I'm thinking about what the team's going to look at it and how we build it out is I reached out to some of my peers at other companies to say, Hey, what are you doing? How are you building your team? How are you splitting the work? Who do you have on your staff? What's your org chart look like? Just questions, questions, questions. And so I think I did probably five or six of those conversations just to really get a sense of what other people were doing. And I think we're all seeing have the same questions, seeing the same challenges, everybody's doing it a little bit differently. I don't think there's any magic answer. And I'm part of a group of people in the industry in a conference every year, and that's a topic of discussion too that we've had there. (00:42:05): And I remember we were having a conversation one day and it was like, what's the answer? And everybody in the call was like, that's what we're figuring it out together. We're the ones that can maybe help create that. But I just dunno that there's ever going to be the right answer. I think it's a constant test and learn, like I said, and every company has different needs and different structures, but I really appreciate and appreciated being able to have those conversations with my peers and just kind of hear what they're doing and hearing that we're having the same question marks and challenges and wins and successes too. So that's pretty fun. And there's one in particular where I actually need to reach out to him. He had offered for us to come and take a look at their studio and I want to go do that. (00:43:00): So just learning. I think I told you when we talked leading up to this conversation about a friend of mine that works at buzzfeed, and we had gone down, we were in LA for vacation, and I was like, Hey, can I come check out your setup? And particularly what they're doing for Tasty. And so we went in, saw their setup for how they were shooting all of those tabletop videos and took that, this is when I was at the agency, but took it back to my team and took lots of pictures and showed them what they were doing, and we set up our own setup to be able to do that. And it was really great. So I think for those of you out there that are listening and doing this and thinking about how to do this, just talk to your colleagues, talk to your peers, learn from what others are doing, listen to podcasts like this. I mean, I'm not an expert. I am learning as I'm going, and all I can do is share my experience. And I think that's, for me, that's a huge thing that I try to do is just learn from others and try things out.", "token_estimate": 676, "speakers": ["Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:41:08", "end_timestamp": "00:41:08", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "eb8c6218-88f4-4e3b-ba41-89c1551315a3", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:44:11): So I think you segued this nicely for folks who are listening, who are asking themselves, if I want to build a studio internally, how do I do that? Clearly it takes a lot of time, it takes a lot of trial and error as you're discovering. If you had to distill down any key lessons or ideas, building an internal team, what are some things that you would suggest people first look at and first start to try to understand or figure out for themselves?\n\nLiv Faris (00:44:40): Yeah, I guess I would say start with your talent. Start with who you have on your team. Understand what they bring to the table. What are their interests? What are their strengths? What's their experience? How can they contribute? And then figure out where your gaps are and then hire for those gaps. So for example, right now, waiting to hear on a couple people out there, but I know that I need somebody to help me with the bringing stuff in-house because most of the people on my team haven't done that. And so I need some support in that. So one of the hires that I'm looking to make now will be, has experience doing that, right? Within another brand. So to your question of before about what are other companies doing and learning from that, it's like, okay, you're working in another brand, you've been doing this, you get what it's like to be in-house, you get what it's like to do this. You have a similar mindset, so hey, let's work together to figure out how we can do that here at Brooks. But I think definitely starting with your internal team and figuring out what it is that they bring to the table and tapping into that talent and experience and skills that they have is huge. And then fill the gaps, like I said,\n\nCole Heilborn (00:46:10): From a leadership perspective, what does it take to lead a studio? Are there, I don't want to say different, but are there any other challenges that people might not anticipate if they're bringing in a studio in-house that they might need to learn and grow on from a leadership perspective?", "token_estimate": 512, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:44:11", "end_timestamp": "00:46:10", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "48cad928-a8cb-4ae4-81b1-b8b58d02568b", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Liv Faris (00:46:33): Yeah, I mean, my big thing with being a manager and a leader is supporting your team, giving them the opportunities to try new things, but also building on their strengths. And so I think that's important, whether it's a studio or any other team, just to make sure that your people are doing well, that they have what they need, are feeling fulfilled and excited about their jobs. Because if you don't have that, you don't have anything, right? If you have a bunch of people coming to work every day that are like, oh, I got to go to my job and I'm not excited about this, that's not great. So that's my number one thing, is just making sure that my team feels fulfilled and that they're not overworked, which can be challenging and giving them what they need. And I think we were having a conversation as a studio leadership team the other day about talking about how some people on the team were working at midnight to get something done. (00:47:41): And one thing at Brooks is that we really value a culture of work-life balance. Get out there, get moving, take time out of your day to take care of your body, to take care of your mental health. And that's really important to us as a company, but there are going to be times when we're going to have to put in some extra hours and things like that. And so I think on this call the other day, it was like, Hey, I want to recognize and give kudos to these people that were doing this because without them it wouldn't have gotten done. And one of the things that brought up for me is coming from an agency is that is going to be, I think, and I can't speak for our whole team, but from my perspective is as we become more of an in-house agency, we're going to have to keep a really good eye on that because anybody that's worked in an agency or knows about agencies, it's like when I worked in an agency, my boss was like, this isn't a 40 hour a week job. (00:48:45): And I think that's why a lot of people get burnt out on agencies because it's just a constant, you're never off. And so I think as we do more work, as we bring more in-house, as we become an agency, we're just going to have to be really mindful of our culture and our people and having enough resources and all of that so that we don't burn people out, and we don't bring in those sort of negative sides of an agency knowing that there will always be times when we're going to have to maybe do a little extra. And then when we do that, how do we recognize that and make sure people are acknowledged for the extra hard work that they're doing? So that's one thing that I see and that became very clear on that call the other day of like, oh, okay, we got to keep an eye on this.", "token_estimate": 680, "speakers": ["Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:46:33", "end_timestamp": "00:46:33", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "f29bd9b9-7645-4d88-83a5-7b5eb2d73e68", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:49:42): Yeah, makes sense. So as you look towards the future, and if you could build any studio that you wanted and finances what weren't a factor, could you describe what that studio looks and feels like? What are they capable of producing?\n\nLiv Faris (00:49:59): Yeah. Well that's interesting. We just recently went and looked at a space that could have potentially been a studio for us, but we weren't quite ready to sign on the dotted line for that one. But we had to look at it from that perspective of, okay, what can we use this space for? And so for us, it's definitely, we have a mix of product shots that we need to get lifestyle type shots. We need to be able to do video. We do some videos where explaining our product that go on our product pages, here's the features of our product, we're doing photography and video. What are needs outside of maybe the creative studio, like our PR team? Do they need a space where our executives can go and do media interviews? Are we going to have a podcast eventually? Do we need an audio booth? I think these are all things that we need to be thinking about and that over the next few months, that's one of the things that we're going to be working on is really understanding what are the needs and how can we meet those. So yeah, I don't know, dream Studio. We'll see.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:51:24): What about from a work perspective? Obviously the space in which you do your work is very important, but if you could describe any sort of deliverable that your studio you would hope they would be able to create in the future, what would that be?", "token_estimate": 404, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:49:42", "end_timestamp": "00:51:24", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "a05accc1-caf3-4ce5-88aa-8e93e8e0b5ea", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Liv Faris (00:51:41): I mean, I love the variety of what we're doing. So I don't know if there's one particular piece. I think everybody loves the storytelling. I think whenever we can do those stories that have a really emotional impact, I think we all feel amazing about that. But I also love seeing the Halloween shot that made people happy and got people excited about our product. So that's just one thing that when I took this job that I was excited about was the variety of things that we're going to be doing. Our sales team, how do we get our sales team excited about our next line of products? We do that through our work. People get a catalog in the mail and they look through the catalog and they feel connected and excited and see themselves in our product. And that all comes from the work that we do. (00:52:45): I think about that when I go into our store. So we have a store for those of you that don't know, we have a store in our headquarters in Fremont called The Trailhead, where you can go in and you can try on our shoes, you can try on our run bras, you can get somebody to help you figure out what's the best for you. And I think about it when I go in there because we have this section that has all of our run brass and up on the wall in that section, our photos of women in our run bras. And I go in there and I'm like, oh, my team took those pictures. And I feel really proud of that. And that is what people see when they come into store. And so I think we're a little bit everywhere when you look, if our CEO is doing a presentation or he's doing a media interview video that we created a B roll compilation last year. So the video that CNN is playing as video that we shot. So I love the variety. So I don't know that I could give you one specific thing that I'd like to do, but I dunno if you asked my team, they might all have something that's on there, list of things they really want to do. But yeah, I love the variety.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:54:07): Makes sense. I mean, that's often why creative people being in the creative field is because there's multiple types of things that you can", "token_estimate": 531, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:51:41", "end_timestamp": "00:54:07", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "4aa42fc2-7610-4d68-9a99-ff03f195741b", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Liv Faris (00:54:14): Do. And I think actually one thing I'll just say on that too is that I, Mike, our VP of creative and I were talking about this the other day of 10, 15 years ago, the opportunity to go work in a brand and create content and do this variety of content like this just maybe didn't exist, right? There were very few companies that were doing that, and now there's more and more opportunities for that. And I know for me, when I was looking for my next thing, to be able to go and tell one story and really have that investment and understanding and love for a brand is huge. And I think maybe people worry that, oh, I'm just going to be telling the same story all the time. But I just feel like there's so many things that we do within the Brooks story. There's so many stories we can tell, there's so many photos we can take. There's so much that we can do. And as the brand evolves and we talk more about respect for the land that we run on and things like that. So I just think the opportunities are really great to come and still have that variety\n\nCole Heilborn (00:55:30): In the future. In an ideal world, again, would you prefer to keep everything?\n\nLiv Faris (00:55:37): I don't think so. No. I think there's always going to be a time to work with an outside partner. I want us to do more, but I think there's always going to be a balance. And just like I said, that testing and learning and figuring out where it works and where it makes sense and where it doesn't. So.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:56:07): So to continue that train of thought, when you do have to make the decision to bring someone in from the outside, how do you make that decision? What are your decision making points? Because that can be a big decision because you're trusting someone, as you said, sometimes the outside partner doesn't get it, get it, they don't quite understand your brand. How do you ensure that the people you do bring in from the outside can actually kind of fall right in line with your internal team?", "token_estimate": 504, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:54:14", "end_timestamp": "00:56:07", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "876003b5-48ce-48ca-9f43-250198480aff", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 26, "content": "Liv Faris (00:56:32): Yeah, I mean, one thing that's been fun that I've seen in some of our pitches and things when we're trying to decide on a partner is that they bring together, every place has somebody that's a runner, whether that's somebody that's running marathons or somebody that goes out for a lunch run three times a week, whatever it may be, they're all runners. And what I've seen is that they bring those people in to work on the teams, and that's huge, just having that mindset. So I think that's one thing that as we sort of review bids and things like that, is just where do they have an understanding of our brand? Where have they done similar work in the past, but also where can their work elevate and maybe change what we've been doing so we're not doing the same thing all the time? (00:57:33): So it's a mix of things. It's definitely how do they approach it? What do they understand of our brand to start with? What does their work look like? What is their project management is a huge, huge thing. Do they get that this needs to stay on schedule and deliver on time and make it easy for us, so we're not struggling to get things. That's huge. So it's a variety of things to look at for sure. And then cost is always a factor too, but really it's most of those other things that are kind of the higher priority in the decision making, I think. Although sometimes it does come down to cost, unfortunately, but we always try to work it out wherever we can.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:58:32): Makes sense. We are coming up on the end of our time for today, but is there anything else that we didn't cover that you feel like is super important to talk about, to wrap us up with?", "token_estimate": 423, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:56:32", "end_timestamp": "00:58:32", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "5ca033d1-ffa8-4d1e-ba5f-5b0b4a987bc0", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 27, "content": "Liv Faris (00:58:48): I mean, for me it's just really that trial and error, figuring it out as you go, tapping into the strengths of your team, giving your team that ownership and excitement over the work that they're doing. Yeah, just try it out. I mean, it can be hard because you have business goals you need to meet. But yeah, I think it's just a big fan of let's try something new and see how it goes and learn from it and do something differently the next time. And not losing sight of your internal talent and everyone, not just your producer or your creative director, but are you having conversation with someone in the lunchroom that has an idea to bring to the table? I think everybody has something to contribute, and I think that's really important and becomes more important as you're doing more and more, right? Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:59:54): Well, it seems like video isn't going away. No, I don't think so. Photos are only going to be needed more and\n\nLiv Faris (01:00:02): More.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:00:02): And so the quest that you're on is frankly very interesting. And again, we've seen a lot of other companies starting to go down this path of bringing things in-house and trying to meet the demands for content and creative and still maintaining that integrity and quality that's needed with each piece of deliverable. And so I appreciate you taking the time to share your process where you're at in the journey. And I think I know that this conversation will be helpful for others who are somewhere along that path for themselves.\n\nLiv Faris (01:00:32): Yeah, I hope so. I mean, I think it's been fun over my career to go from where video was not really taken as seriously, I guess, or not as important in the mix of things to now where it's everywhere. So it's been interesting to see, and I just love being able to build a team and give people opportunities and try things out and deal with that crisis moment when it comes up.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:01:02): But it sounds like again, you thrive in those situations. So really if leave is on your team, there's no stress.", "token_estimate": 524, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:58:48", "end_timestamp": "01:01:02", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "ed023515-dd2c-41ac-9bd8-6df26b3ace2f", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 28, "content": "Liv Faris (01:01:09): Oh, I don't know about that fall apart. I do my best. Yeah. But yeah, thank you, Cole. I really appreciate you having me on and hearing my perspective. And like I said, I'm no expert, but hopefully some of you that are listening or take something away even one little nugget is then I've done my job, right?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:01:31): Absolutely. Yeah. And if Pope people want to follow up with you, if they have any questions, where can they go to connect with you and where can they follow along with the Brooks content that your internal team is crushing?\n\nLiv Faris (01:01:42): Yeah, I mean, I think the best way to reach me is probably through LinkedIn is the best way, and then follow our YouTube channel. We're doing more and more content on YouTube. Follow our Instagram. Yeah, follow our TikTok, come to our store. If you're in Seattle, go to a specialty retailer and go try on a pair of shoes. See, I was in North Dakota with my family vacation and I went into a retailer there and there was our photos on display with our shoes. So it's everywhere. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:02:23): Awesome. Well thank you again for taking the time. I hope you have a great rest of your day. And yeah, I'm very curious to see how 2023 shakes out and definitely want to check back in and see how the continuation of building the team is going.\n\nLiv Faris (01:02:36): Yeah, definitely. We'll need to check in later this year. See how it looks. Alright, thanks again. Bye. Bye.", "token_estimate": 373, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:01:09", "end_timestamp": "01:02:36", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "f88b999b-147c-4cc4-8f0b-8b1b5a02f94c", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Ryan Coulter (00:00:00): A common leadership failure is in trying to over control your team, you're trying to enable those people and you're trying to let them figure out where their limits are so you're an advocate for them and you're going to push on them in a way like a coach of mine for them to figure out what they're really capable of.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:28): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Ryan Coulter. He's the founder and chief creative officer of the James Brand. Ryan, how are you?\n\nRyan Coulter (00:00:38): I'm good, Cole. Hey, nice to talk to you.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:40): Yeah, good to have you on the show today. Looks like it's a busy day in your life, taking this from the car.\n\nRyan Coulter (00:00:47): Yeah, sometimes the car is actually the quietest, warmest, most secure place to get things done. Our office is busy and has a lot of concrete, and so sometimes these things are actually better done in here than up there.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:01): I like it. Yeah, it's a good sound studio you've got.\n\nRyan Coulter (00:01:04): Yeah, well, damped should manage our audio well.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:09): Awesome. Where are you calling in from?\n\nRyan Coulter (00:01:11): I'm in Portland, Oregon. I'm actually in the northeast corner. We were doing a photo shoot over here at to coffee shop this morning and we're in there getting caffeinated and getting some things set up and so yeah, I'm here in the beautiful Pacific Northwest.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:25): Awesome. Well, I would love if you could give us a quick introduction to the James brand. I've only discovered it since connecting with you and have been pretty blown away by I feel like what I was missing out on. And so I'd love if you could give our listeners a breakdown of the company and who you guys are.", "token_estimate": 466, "speakers": ["Ryan Coulter", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:00:00", "end_timestamp": "00:01:25", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "d104e6d3-4fdf-423b-a16a-e4e914dc2f01", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Ryan Coulter (00:01:42): Yeah, so we are the James brand. We've been around, at least on paper since 2011. We didn't really get things into the market until halfway through 2014 and didn't really get more serious about the business till about 2017. So it's been kind of a long slow burn, but it started for me, I'm the founder, so this was kind of my doing initially, but it was a project that I was working on in that I was a person that had always carried a pocket knife and a few other things in my pockets. My general triad was a field notes notebook in my back pocket, a pen, usually a cheap disposable pen and a pocket knife. And I used those three things all the time, every day for lots of various tasks. Sometimes they were really mundane cleaning out my fingernail or slicing up an apple at lunch. (00:02:36): Sometimes they were more serious like cutting a rope when you needed to cut it or something that actually had higher demands. But I was just kind of blown away at how utilitarian those objects were and how important they were in my life. And the real asset test for anything like that is when you forget it or you lose it or you leave it and you start to reach for it and it's not there and you're like, oh man, I really wish I had that with me. And I thought that that as an idea was a really interesting idea and there really wasn't a brand that was focused on that and telling that story. And a lot, particularly in the pocketknife space, most of the incumbent brands were very focused kind of in the realm of tactical applications, a lot of military contracts, and there's a lot of blacked out stuff and more weapons point of view or fairly traditional hook and bullet outdoor hunting and fishing applications. (00:03:36): And I think there's some great companies and they do a great job with that. But no one was really telling a story of, Hey, here's some fairly minimal design language. This one is really just designed to live on top of your dresser. This is the one you should kind of grab every day when you roll out the door and just use it for no one particular purpose, but just for those 10 little things that are going to show up throughout the day. And I was like, wow, that's a kind of interesting opportunity. In reality, most people I think were using these products to do that, even if they were buying them for other emotional purposes. (00:04:12): So that was kind of the brand positioning thinking. But from the product standpoint, the things that I like, I'm an industrial designer by trade washed up industrial designer, and so a lot of what I like and a lot of what I tried to do in my design work and in my work as a creative director was really try to clean things up and make them very minimal and be very reductive and get rid of everything you didn't need and sort of see what kind of essence was left. And especially back in 2011, in that period of time, a lot of the design language in the pocket knife category was very overbuilt and lots of big camo handles and lots and lots of exposed fasteners, and there was just a lot of adding of things to the products and not taking things away. And I was always like Joni Ives fan, a Deida ROMs fan. (00:05:12): I really liked the minimalism in trying to reduce things, but no one had really done much of that in this category. So we set out to say, Hey, if the brand positioning is about daily care, can the product positioning be about minimizing these things and actually reducing them down to their bare essence and just seeing what's left. So that's kind of how we started. And in the spirit, if you can't find what you're looking for, you might have to go make it yourself. We started drawing things. We started working with some folks to try to get some things made. All of the early days stuff was terrible. It took a lot longer to figure it out, but it really was a nights and weekends project. And it was somewhat selfish initially because I was really trying to come up with this pocket knife that I wanted that was in my head that I wanted to carry with me every day.", "token_estimate": 1019, "speakers": ["Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:01:42", "end_timestamp": "00:01:42", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "f1e12a4f-5d0e-4ec4-b5c9-e153cf5772da", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:06:08): When did the everyday carry category become its own category? I feel like I've only been aware of it in the last 10 years.\n\nRyan Coulter (00:06:17): And I think that timeline is about right. I mean, I didn't really know much about the category until about that timeline either. And I don't know how I stumbled upon it, but there was, the website still is the site. We do some business with them every day. carry.com, that was really, it just featured laydowns, like these are the things that people are carrying with them every day. And it was generally a photo of a pocket dump or a lay down and then a list of what those products were and then a bit of an explanation about who the person was and a little bit, at least about why they were making those decisions. And I was just blown away that that was a thing too. I was like, wait, there's a category. This thing has a name, there's a website around it. I had no idea. (00:07:04): I never thought about that at all. And so it was the first time for me again about that same timeframe, about 2010 to really see the category with the name and start to see other people doing this and thinking about it and curating the stuff and actually taking photos. So I think it's been, I'm sure people that are more versed in it than you or I would say that it's been around longer, but I think that's when I first noticed it. I do know that a little bit of the history in that space was really sort of tactically focused a lot around police and sort of special operators and people really trying to say, Hey, here are the things, there are a lot more firearms in the space in those days. And people were saying like, Hey, here are the things that I bring into these really high risk situations to best manage my risk. (00:08:02): And it was really a lot of information exchange around the decisions people were making around the kind of sidearm they would carry into a special operations mission or that a particular police officer or military officer may choose and why they were choosing it. And there was a very functional, I mean these people were trying to stay alive and keep them other people alive and were spending time sort of exchanging the ideas around that. And so it evolved from that into something that was a very practical application into something that became a little bit more emotional. And I was really enamored and still am, I think with this idea that these decisions that you're making and what you're curating and bringing with you can be very much a reflection of who you are and what your values are and how you spend your time. And that ability to move through 30 different lay downs in everyday carry and say, that person's interesting. That person's interesting. Wait a minute, this person is just like me. I can make some guesses about this person. And they're probably involved in creative endeavors and they like to go outdoors on weekends. And I just thought it was amazing how much you could at least philosophically read into a person and their motivations by seeing the things that they actually brought with them day in and day out. And I still think it's just a really fun concept to explore.", "token_estimate": 801, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:06:08", "end_timestamp": "00:06:17", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "7b0b9077-8096-4903-af28-35d28bb55d6e", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:09:33): So let's rewind a little bit and I want to spend just a little bit of time in the past because I know you've got quite the history of different career stops along the way before you've ended up with the James brand or starting the James brand. Could you give us the quick highlight reel of where you've been?\n\nRyan Coulter (00:09:49): Yeah, the highest highlight reels, I've spent a lot of my career at Burton Snowboards and at Nike with a lot of other things along the way. My career has kind of triangulated between larger corporate work, some startup and entrepreneurial work, and then consulting, sort of design and innovation consulting. And I've kind of rotated through all of those things at various times and they all kind of feed each other and keep me from getting bored. But initially, I grew up in the Midwest. I was a skateboarder since early, since I was five. I'm still a skateboarder, and that was my first love. And that led to snowboarding in the Midwest because we were really just looking for a way to skateboard in the winter where it was cold. And back in those you really had to hang your skateboard up in about November in Indiana, and you may not get it back out until spring because it was just cold and snowy and icy and there were no such thing as indoor skate parks or skate parks of any kind. And so it was really hard to hang it up and stop. I mean, I think it's more a 365 activity now, but we really had to just stop. And so we were looking for ways to continue to do that. Snowboarding was kind of showing up and believe it or not, even in south central Indiana, there are still some tiny little ski resorts. Paoli Peaks is where I learned it, still operated there and actually just got bought by Veil, I think, which is amazing. (00:11:24): So I was really into snowboarding and really into skateboarding. I went to college at Purdue. I was a mechanical engineering student first. It's an Indiana school, and I grew up really wanting to make stuff. I always drew things and I'm not really an artist. I never was. I was more on the technical side and I used a lot of straight edges and circle templates, and I drew a trillion spaceships and airplanes and other things, but I was always very interested in the idea of just making these things. I was drawing a reality from a very early age. I was into that and tried to do that. And so I became a mechanical engineer engineering student because I thought that's how you made stuff. I was in my second year of that at Purdue, and I ran into an exhibition in the Union of Industrial Design, and I never even heard of Industrial Design, but here were all these really interesting scale models of cars and devices and all these beautiful renderings and technical drawings at this point, kind of pre-computer, but done by hand. And I was like, wait a minute. This is what I always thought I was going to do. I'm actually doing sewage calculations and hydrodynamics and what is this? Is this a thing? And so I totally fell in love with the concept of industrial design, switched my major to industrial design. And then some of my final college projects were actually snowboarding projects. (00:12:52): I got connected with Vans and did a really early sort of step in boot binding combination for Vans back in I think 1995 with a guy named Kim Boyle, who I always credit is kind of giving me a chance at a really early age. I can't even remember how we got connected, but he makes amazing custom motorcycles now. But I've always remembered Kim as a person that gave me sort of a shot. So I did that with Vans way back when I got my first job after college working for a small snowboard company in Colorado called Solid Up in the Hills up in Fairplay. And my nickname there was college. I had two nicknames. College, I was the guy around there who had been to college, which made everyone assume that I could fix everything and solve everything. And in reality, I was no more qualified to do that than any of the other people that were there. (00:13:49): Or they called me Steaks too. A lot of people still call me that because I was this sort of pale guy from the Midwest and moved to Colorado and I had the world's craziest goggle tan that made me looked like marbled meat because just white eyes and red face. And I was the color of steak the entire time I lived there, but working for solid for a bit. And then I ended up in California very shortly working for Joyride and doing some other stuff in kind of the southern California business. And then I got a call and got recruited by Burton, and that had always been the dream job, the dream company, and the job was to actually be the product manager for snowboard bids for Burton, which was very literally my dream job. I mean, that was the job that if I could have had any job in the world I would've had, and I got it, which was amazing. (00:14:49): And so in my early twenties, I moved to Vermont and started working for Burton and was there for about almost eight years working mostly on snowboard bindings and then on a few other things. And then as a part of my time at Burton, I spent a chunk of every summer out here in Oregon at Mount Hood testing next year's gear, and really fell in love with Oregon and your ability to do anything at any time. Those days when I would come out here with Burton, you would get picked up by a bunch of people that were already out here. You would go to Burnside to the skate park and skate immediately, and then you'd drive up to the mountain and you'd be snowboarding for days on end in a t-shirt or a sweatshirt, and then you'd get tired of doing that every day. So then you'd drive to the coast to go surfing, and then we'd drive into town and see the pixies and all these things that for me, I had always wanted to do in the Midwest. (00:15:51): And you couldn't do, we didn't have skate parks, we didn't have a beach, we didn't really have mountains, and we surely didn't have the pixies, but here they were all literally within an hour and a half of each other. And I was just smitten with the ability to do any of that stuff. So I left Burton, I came out to work for Nike for, I've worked for Nike for a total of 10 years, kind of in two different stints, left to do an earlier startup called Uncommon, which was some of the world's first fully customizable iPhone cases, and then did some design consulting, went back to Nike for a while to continue to work on customization and the Nike ID platform, and then was doing the James brand through kind of nights and weekends and then made the commitment to do this full time just a few years ago. And here we are.", "token_estimate": 1667, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:09:33", "end_timestamp": "00:09:49", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "807a1bb5-8ccd-4fde-8da3-3da6eedd264a", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:16:49): Here we are. So I'm really curious, the theme of our episode today is all about leadership and leading creatives and being a leader of a creative team. I'm curious, when did you first realize that you were a leader? Did it hit you one day when everything changed? You're like, oh, shoot, I have this responsibility, I have this role. Was there a moment that you remember?\n\nRyan Coulter (00:17:16): Yeah, it's a great question. There is no one moment that I remember, but I think part of it was the good news and bad news about working for some of these companies, Burton in particular, and Nike is I was absolutely working with and around some of the very best talent in the world, the very highest levels of talent in the world. And I was not that, I'm not that still, but there's a lot that I have to offer. But I was starting to see this sort of multiplying effect of my ability to find those people and organize them around an idea and sort of bring them together to make this symphony of really amazing work. And that to me was wildly rewarding because it was clearly both in quality and quantity, it was stuff that I couldn't do myself. But one of the things about a lot of top talent like that is that they couldn't do it by themselves either. (00:18:27): It required more breadth, they required lots of direction and shaping of the thing and making it into an idea that you could actually sell into corporate leadership and a story around it. In a lot of ways, my primary skillset was verbal was written. I was able to kind of pull together these story arcs and stitch together a lot of individual things into a bigger tale. And so I think I started to see that sort of really for the first time when I started working for Burton, and I'm responsible for this. I mean, the thing about Burton, and I'm sure it's still the case, and I credit Jake for this, it was sink or swim man, I'm 23 or something, and I'm responsible basically for the majority of the snowboard binding business, which was tens and tens of millions of dollars at that point. I've never worked for a company and I've got a design degree, but I'm fairly fresh out of college and they're like, figure it out or leave. (00:19:38): It's up to you. We believe in you, but you're going to have to do this. And so suddenly I was kind of, I think the first industrial designer that Burton had ever hired. But we started working, I think Burton was already doing this, but working with design consultants in Boston, a firm called 11 and a few other folks. And so suddenly my job became managing this design process instead of doing the work myself, I started really managing this process and the budgets around it and working with these really talented people who may not even understand snowboarding and may not understand Burton culture and may not understand manufacturing, but working with them to get solutions that would work for all of those things for the athletes, something that was going to make Craig Kelly happy and Terry happy, and something that we could actually manufacture and something that would meet the price points for the sales team. (00:20:33): But I was good at that. I was able to sort of balance all those things and get some really interesting work done. And again, the quality of the work, I would brief a project and we would go look at the first round, I would just be amazed at how good it was and just the quality of it, but it was within a set of guidelines that I was drawing and that I knew would work. And so that for me, it was powerful to look, oh man, you can do some stuff that's highly valuable. They couldn't do it without you. You couldn't do it without them. So it really started to happen. Then that's when it started to really click for me.", "token_estimate": 946, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:16:49", "end_timestamp": "00:17:16", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "cd71515b-1cb1-4f20-98ec-41ed4ee438e6", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:21:08): So based on that, I'm curious to hear what your definition of a leader is. Well, I'll stop there. What is the definition of a leader to you?\n\nRyan Coulter (00:21:17): Well, I mean I think as a leader you really have two jobs and it's motivated and trained, train, train and motivate. And so motivation is helping people understand what the goal is and where you're trying to go and what the higher level vision is for the entire thing you're working on, and then what their part of that is. And so on one hand you have to kind of paint this north star of like, Hey, we're all pulling over here and we're going to do this and it's going to be epic, but it's going to be a long journey, but this is where we're going. Do you see that North star? Yeah, yeah, I see it. Okay, so you see it and you're clear on it and you get it. Okay, so your part of that looks like this. You need to do this part, and that's your part of getting us here, and then it will follow it up with this and this. So I think to motivate people, you really have to balance these two interesting ideas of painting this kind of far away vision of what success and victory looks like and then saying, okay, well if you believe that the step you have to take today is this and the step you have to take tomorrow may be this. And so keep going back and forth between those two different polls in terms of the timeline and the vision. (00:22:31): And then on the training side, you need to make sure that everyone understands that they have the skills that they need to do that and that they have the confidence that they need to do that and that they are technically able to do it. People in general, I think in both academic and physical endeavors underestimate themselves. They don't have the confidence and they don't necessarily know what they really can or can't do all the time. So you want to train them about what they're actually capable of and what they're not capable of. They need to understand for themselves where those lines are. But I think a leader is a person that can manage both of those things. And I think we're just having a leadership conversation this morning with somebody that's working on some HR issues and just really being empathetic and trying to know the people that you're leading and really understand what motivates them and who they are in the world and how to get things done is really interesting. (00:23:34): I think a lot of my years, my title has been creative director, and I often think that you should switch words around, it's like you're a director, director of creative is a word that gets thrown around a lot because I think I've never seen anyone more creative than construction workers who actually solve real problems on the fly. And so labeling anything as a creative endeavor, like the work we do, I think is a disservice to almost everybody because I think real work and almost any rewarding work is creative. Plumbers are wildly creative. Watch them figure out how to make something work in your house that was broken. But I think when you put director in front, director of whatever it is that you want to be doing, it changes your orientation, but you're trying to direct a symphony of these people to work together with all of their different idiosyncrasies and motivations and egos to produce this one thing. (00:24:36): And if you can get some success in that with them and get them to see the result of that, it's wildly motivating to all of them because like, oh my gosh, I didn't know we could do that. That work far exceeds the sum of its parts. I didn't know I could do that, and I didn't know if we all pulled this together that we were able to do that and suddenly magic will start to happen. And so I think my favorite analogy for someone who's good at that as a rule is the conductor of a symphony. The show doesn't happen without them. They control the tone, they control the timing, they control where it goes, but they're not your first chair violinist. I mean, that person is a specialist, a virtuoso, but the violinist can't do it without the conductor, and the conductor can't do it without the violinist, but the conductor has to manage that entire orchestra and all of those egos and people and know the difference between the bass player and the guy playing the Tim drums in the back. (00:25:35): It's a very broad thing that he's working on. It's very personal. He needs to understand the motivations and the differences in all those people. So in reality, a lot of directing of any kind, creative directing or any kind is working with people. It's the management of people and it spend Nike, I spent so much of my time having coffee with people. Nike has very much this matrix culture, but so much of my time was spent having coffee with people and trying to get them to talk to me about where they were, how the work is going, what they're feeling, what they need, where they're struggling, trying to help them. And if you're doing that, you're really trying hard to get out of the way of your team and let them do their thing and making sure everyone has enough. Everyone should have enough sort of tolerance that they can scratch and dent the car, but they can't total it. (00:26:32): You can always take a dent out and we can always get a little bit repainted. But I think a common leadership failure is in trying to over control your team, you're trying to enable those people and you're trying to let them figure out where their limits are. So you're an advocate for them and you're going to push on them in a way like a coach might for them to figure out what they're really capable of. But if you're in their way too much, much, much, you will demotivate those people. So you have to let them explore and go on their own and do your part to make sure that it stays within the boundaries, but it may get really close to your boundaries, and that's why the boundaries, you should let it get close, but then know when to stop it. So I think that whole process to me is just, I've spent a lot of time, I've read as many books on this subject as I can. I try to pass it on. I try to be, I consider that my best professional skill, and I've tried really hard to be a professional at doing that. Again, I've consumed the literature and tried hard to trade myself to be a very effective creative leader because that for me is I think where I do my best work.", "token_estimate": 1608, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:21:08", "end_timestamp": "00:21:17", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "1560b4ff-dd00-4af5-bb04-674dd2ceba11", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:27:51): So many years ago I was in Boy Scouts and we were learning about the three types of leadership, and there was this little graph that I remember reading about. There was the person who's in front of the group leading everyone. There's the person who's in the middle of the group leading from amidst the team, and then there's the person who's behind the group pushing everyone forwards. And I think that's a very simplified way of looking at it. But when I think about leadership and I think about leadership in today's culture, I feel like the type of leadership that we imagine is the person out in front who's leading the charge, who's very, very disciplined, very decisive, very, very driven. And I'm curious what your perspective is or where you feel like you fit into, not necessarily those three examples, but if you have a different leadership style than maybe the cultural described.", "token_estimate": 230, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:27:51", "end_timestamp": "00:27:51", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "6cf1883f-ec1d-4e82-9cd2-bca1234d99dd", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Ryan Coulter (00:28:48): Yeah, I think the media loves the story of the leaders out in front. The media loves the Elon Musks and these big powerful shaping figures, but I think it's a bit of a myth, and I think there aren't statues to committees. It's just a much more palatable story to like, oh, this person just reading an article in the Wall Street Journal this morning about Joni Ive and how powerful Joni Ive has been for Apple and for culture. And that's true, he has been. But Joni, ive has teams of hundreds of people that actually work under him. And so he doesn't do all of that much on his own. He's been able to work with all of these other people that do it, but if your average person were to read that, you would think it's like one man sitting by himself just banging that out or beating these other people to do it. (00:29:44): But it's not like that. And I don't think he works like that. I mean, I think it's actually him motivating this and building this team of hundreds of really, really good people and enabling them to do their best work, establishing the guidelines, making it clear the kind of things that the company wants and needs, but then leading from the back. And so I do think I try to be a person that leads from the back, and there are oftentimes I will let things go that I don't think are great or that I don't think are necessarily right for my personal opinion, but I'll let them go because they're motivating for this person. They feel like that's good work, it's not going to do any harm. And it's not about me enforcing my will on anyone, even on the brand, even on a brand that I started. (00:30:31): It's more important to let people go and see what the market or other people may say about things. So I think leading from the front is valuable, mostly from the perspective of making sure people are aligned and understand where we're going. But the second you start crossing the line of telling people how they're going to get there, probably going too far, they need to tell you how they're going to get there, and you need to help them with that and coach them and guide 'em. But you really want to be supportive and empathetic and an enabler of them to do their best work. I mean, I think it's a critical skill for any of these companies to be building people and making them better and challenging them and getting them to become good leaders too, and let them start to see the multiplying effects of doing that. (00:31:24): So it's a long answer to a short question, but I do try to lead from the back, although I do have to go up front and just make sure everyone is squared up of where we're going. That's just a role that I have, but then I try to go to the back and let people do what they're going to do and help 'em out with that. Again, I spend a lot of my time talking with people. I spend the majority of my time, even now at a small company, is in meetings. And a lot of that is in guiding people and trying to help them and just kind of shepherding things along. I mean, it's a shepherding role. The sheep dog is running behind these sheep, and the sheep are all doing their own thing. Some of 'em are going fast, some of 'em are going slow, but we're all going in the same place. And my job is to run around from the back. I got to go run up front and have someone open the gate so we know where we're going, and then I got a zigzag back and forth and get everyone in, get the straggler in, get the other person in, and they're all going to do it their own way, but we'll all end up there together.", "token_estimate": 891, "speakers": ["Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:28:48", "end_timestamp": "00:28:48", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "44ab5544-1161-4795-a784-c926026b803e", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:32:20): So you mentioned some of the key elements of leadership for you are setting the vision and then training people and motivating people to reach that vision. I'm curious if there's any other principles that you have learned or adopted along the way that you kind of keep your quiver of tools, anything stand out?\n\nRyan Coulter (00:32:43): I mean, we talked a little bit about it before, but this idea of not telling people how to do their job, telling them what your expectations are and then letting them figure out how to do the job but not micromanaging them. I think that it's so easy for people, directors, creative directors or people that are very vested in the concept to do that and think that I can't think that I have the answer again. Even with a company that I started, I can't think that I have the answer. It's a brand made up of people and it's the amalgamation of all of their ideas. And so I think it's a very difficult task to step back and let people do things, even if I think those things are a mistake, even if those things may, and again, this is the words of Ray Dalio and his book principles, but even if those things will scratch and dent the car, let 'em scratch and dent it, we'll all learn something from that and we can fix that and we won't do that again. (00:33:45): But then also knowing that, hey, if you're going to total the car, I have to pull over and we have to stop this. And so I think not micromanaging, really letting people tell you how they're going to do the work and then working with them to figure out if they need training to learn how to do something they don't know how to do, I think is really important. But I think there's this very simplified idea that your job as a leader is really only twofold, motivating and training motivate and train, train and motivate. And those are big words, and you can unpack a lot of them, but that can really simplify your approach. And when you sit down with someone to go do a one-on-one or talk to them, I'm trying to figure out what do they need right now? Are they burned out? Are they having a hard time at home? (00:34:35): Do they not understand the vision? Well, those are motivation problems. What can I do to help with that? Or do they not understand something? Do they need a skill reinforced? Do they need training on what our expectations are? So I think those principles have really helped simplify this for me to help really make it easier. And when I'm training younger leaders and people who are just starting this, it's very overwhelming for people, especially designers and other people who've been working on their own to start trying to do this with other people. And the number one thing you see happen or that I often see happen is instead of working hard to get the work they want to get out of others, they end up just doing it themselves. No, now I'll just do it. I'll just take it back and do it. And often that's the faster way to do it at first, but that's the wrong way to do it. (00:35:31): You'll never have any multiplying effects until you can get that person to do the work the way you them to do it. And so breaking that habit for young leaders is really important, and it's really hard because again, they would've been more efficient just to do it on their own. Now they've got this new person to manage, and it's suddenly more inefficient, which is big shock to people because it's got a new person. Things are going to go twice as fast. It's like they're going to go slower twice as slow for a while, and then they'll go 25% faster and you're going to spend 25% of your time managing that person. But telling them, Hey, before you get overwhelmed with this concept of leadership and leading new people, it's motivation and training, training and motivation. Talk to them and figure out what they need and then figure out how you can give that to them. You're their advocate and you're an enabler and you have two jobs. Motivate and train, train and motivate.", "token_estimate": 1003, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:32:20", "end_timestamp": "00:32:43", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "7ffe0dcc-55e6-4e9f-8ccb-c27e4baac581", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:36:31): All right, so this is timely. I want to dive a little deeper into the idea of training. I've been reading this book, I've been listening to this book. It's an audible called Do Hard Things. Have you read it by chance?\n\nRyan Coulter (00:36:41): No. No, I haven't.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:36:43): I can't remember the title or I can't remember the author's name. But basically the premise, well so far I'm like three chapters in is all about the importance of doing hard things and how we do hard things. And so far the book has opened up on the idea that he uses a lot of sport. The author uses a lot of sport or military examples. And basically the idea is in sports in order to be successful, coaches will just throw people into the deep end and expect them to come out on top or rise to the challenge and have kind of figured out how to perform at an elite level. And the author makes a point to say that rarely do we actually give people in sports training or the tools to actually reach that elite level. The training often doesn't come with the expectations, and he uses the example of the military and he uses like hell week with the seals as an example. (00:37:46): Most people just assume that SEAL training is to find the best of the best, but a lot of his perspective is yes, it's to find the best of the best, but they've already been trained on how to become the best. And so they have the tools to do so. And a lot of expectations from sports teams is we expect them to be the best, but we don't give them the tools to do so. We expect them to figure it out on their own. How important for you is the training component and how do you view training? Is that you're showing someone specifically how to use a program? Is it teaching someone philosophy of how do you think through that?", "token_estimate": 455, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:36:31", "end_timestamp": "00:36:43", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "db5079a3-29ad-4bfb-b6ed-f0b45a58aa52", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Ryan Coulter (00:38:25): Yeah, it's a really good question because I think of those two, it's the one that actually in the world of motivation and training that needs the most exploration, a lot of training, to me it's very rarely is it technical like, oh, you don't know how to do this. Sometimes it is and it's like, Hey, well then you need to get a class. There are things you can do. And that's the easiest in some ways, problem to fix. But a lot of training is more about communication and expectations for communication and expectation management and like, Hey, in order for this work relationship to be highly functional, I need to actually know some things about you. I'm not asking for your personal information, but I need you to have a pretty honest conversation with yourself about who you are and what motivates you and where you want to be in the world. (00:39:18): And so a lot of training is about the dialogue that I expect to have with people. For example, I do a one-on-one meeting with a direct report. We generally try to do it once a week for one hour. And I don't always live up to this necessarily, but you can't be late if you're five minutes late, it's canceled and I won't go to it unless before we actually go, you've written out an agenda like what you want to cover, and I need that 24 hours before the meeting starts because I need time to actually reciprocate because I will go and look things up, I will pull references, I will get links, I will do my work, and that meeting will be productive. So these one-on-ones become really important, but there's this training component of my expectations for how to make them impactful. And so the person I'm working with has to live up to their end of the bargain and show their level of investments with getting the scheduling right, doing their prep work, doing the prep work on the right time so I can do it. (00:40:26): But then we've trained each other for how to do those. And I think almost everybody that does those sort of with me is those are incredibly important meetings. And we don't talk about work at all the work of the work. We don't talk about talk, how we do the work and working with other people and their motivations and what's going on in their life to the level that they're comfortable with so that I can help them. And so there's training there for what a one-on-one meeting means and how we're going to do it. And I think it's not stuff that I invented, I learned that from other people and doing this stuff, but it's training in that this is how we should work together. This is the most efficient way for us to get the most out of each other. And this is how you can use me as a resource. I'm your advocate. I'm here to make you better, making you better makes me better, makes the brand better. NPS, I get things out of these meetings too. I learn a lot about myself, it makes me better. So let's train. That's training. (00:41:26): And I was just talking about this literally in this meeting right before our call, but the general sort of standard that I think is good for people to be working at is slight discomfort. And it's kind of like the concept of target heart rate where it's like, I don't want your heart rate at resting, and I don't want it at 160 like Max, I want you to work at about one 20 all the time. So you should be stretching and uncomfortable. And the more you do that, the more you'll get done and the more you'll be capable of. But you shouldn't coast and you shouldn't burn out, but you should feel a little bit of tension in everything you do. And that's in work and life. I mean, I just believe that as a truism, but I use that as an expectation of how I expect people to be, which is a little bit of motivation and also a little bit of training in terms of training can be a lot about expectation management. (00:42:24): This is what I expect out of you, this is what I expect you to do. This is kind of how I expect that to go. I'm not going to really tell you too much about how to do it, but if you are consistent in your training of what you expect out of people, they will start to internalize that and understand it and start to deliver on it, and then you can continue to push them on that. I mean, I'm a personal trainer of your professional performance and I'm going to continually press you to get better. I mean, it's a very important thing. I'm in my early fifties and Mike Fer, our CEO and business partner, he's in his fifties. We've done a lot of this stuff and it's really, really important for us that the James brand is a company that is making our employees better. (00:43:15): We want to make the world better, we want to make a lot of things better, but we are both consciously trying to leverage our experience, hopefully our wisdom or just our years in the seat to make people better. For what it's worth, I mean, clearly you're dealing with a few different people's opinions and experiences, but that's part of that dialogue is like, do you think you're getting smarter? Do you know more now than you did before you came here? Could you get a job that's higher paying with a better title right now if you walked out of here because of the things that we're doing together? That's the journey we're on. We're committed to that personal development and growth. But a lot of that does come down to training of expectations and what people should be expecting out of themselves. What they're capable of is a part of training. And their motivation is like, now you know what you're capable of, how are you going to get there? How are you going to do it? So it's interesting how so many of these things become soft science and really personal science, but that's just the nature of working with teams. And again, directing means working with great teams of people. That's what the job is.", "token_estimate": 1460, "speakers": ["Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:38:25", "end_timestamp": "00:38:25", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "9e55cd0a-6e90-4dc2-90b2-3471c42fbfc6", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:44:33): I'm curious if you could share a breakdown of how the average day, how you spend your time is how much of it is spent actually doing the work? How much of it is actually spent navigating the team dynamic? What's that breakdown look like? Just in the average day,\n\nRyan Coulter (00:44:55): I would say probably 20% of my time is actually making stuff. And I read a lot of the copy for the James Red. I have a lot of actual production responsibilities because we are still a small brand. We got a lot to do, but I spend the rest of the 80% of my time in meetings with various people inside, outside direct reports, et cetera, trying to keep things flowing, keep things moving through the pipeline, reviewing work as a creative director or whatever. I do spend a lot of my time reviewing work and making comments about it and sort of shepherding that work through the gate. But it's funny, I actually try hard to divorce myself from doing too much actual the production of creative work. And I see it as a failure if I'm doing too much of it and that I haven't properly delegated work and that I don't have the right resources to do it. (00:46:01): And sometimes I think I'm a good at writing copy, for example, and I write a lot of it for the brand. I probably have a good, maybe the best handle on that, but I don't always love doing it because in order to really do it well, I actually need to be in the right mindset. I need to sit down in the right setting and really focus on it. And it's very difficult to do that in the office and during business hours. So I may well do that at nights or early mornings or on the weekend and I'm like, Hey, that's actually not that efficient. You should probably have somebody else get somebody else who can do it. It's a thing that actually keeps me up at night right now and that we haven't yet been able to get other folks within the brand who can handle that kind of storytelling yet. (00:46:50): It's a limitation. The company and the brand should absolutely be able to go on without me or without any of the key players because things should be structured and formulaic and sort of built enough that they're scalable. So I never want to be a bottleneck on that. And that means doing less of the actual creative work, like the actual production of that and more of the management. So it's probably 80 20 right now, and I do some, but I'll probably do less as we move on. The ideal role is probably I should do of it, and I should be working with a team that's doing a hundred percent of it that I think are just amazing and that are blowing my mind and I'm kind of guiding it and I present that stuff to the company or media pers or whatever. And I'm incredibly proud of it. (00:47:45): I mean, I look at it, I'm just like, wow, look what we've done. And it's interesting because it's like, well, what did you do in there, Ryan? Did you do any of that? I'm like, well, no. And yes, I directed it. I wrote the breeze for it, I pulled it together. I made that team app and I enabled them. I guided it. So it wouldn't happen without me. It wouldn't have that vision without me. But I also didn't click a shutter. I didn't draw a sketch. So that's the interesting dynamic of I did it, but I didn't do it. But I think ideally it would get to the point where I didn't do any of it, but I surely enabled all of it and helped make all of it happen.", "token_estimate": 853, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:44:33", "end_timestamp": "00:44:55", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "e80c4966-bb8a-4acf-b918-29dd86fe2e53", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:48:24): So I know I'm going to use the word creative only because it's to define a group of people. I know that everyone is creative, as we talked about earlier. Is there anything different about leading a creative team? Do they need different types of support? Do they need different types of motivations or vision versus a quote non-creative team? And I guess I'm thinking creative in terms of graphic design, copywriting, film, photography, something more in the arts.", "token_estimate": 123, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:48:24", "end_timestamp": "00:48:24", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "6442ee41-eb2b-4d79-bdb3-630f6e81f738", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Ryan Coulter (00:48:59): So a couple of interesting answers on that. I did a class, a general management class at a place called Harrison Metal in San Francisco that was led by this guy named Michael Deering, who was an ex eBay executive and really one of the most just amazing, smartest, most engaging people I've ever met. Wildly inspiring. And this was a three day course that was initially designed for a lot of the San Francisco tech entrepreneurial startup crowd with good ideas about how to manage a business, like basic performance reviews and a lot of the things, some of the things that we're talking about. And they had a section in there about the management of engineers and artists. And the key takeaway, and I've internalized this, I believe it was that you don't manage those people differently. That is a line that we're sold in some ways to make the creative sets seem maybe more elites than they actually are. (00:50:02): And that what matters in the management of people, whether it's an apprentice plumber or a designer too, is really just understanding their motivations and their skills. And so the short answer is no. I don't believe there's any difference in that whatsoever. And I think any real efforts to apply that are either falsely the importance or the difficulty of creative work versus anything else and or undermining the actual creative work that everybody does, point blank. I don't believe you can do happy work of any kind unless it's creative. I think that is the thing that motivates everybody. But I think I've worked jobs that were menial the most menial, but I found creative ways to solve those problems. I found the most efficient way to clean the rack at the pizza place that I worked, and I was interested in finding the best way to do it, the most efficient way to do it. (00:51:02): But that was a creative endeavor, and I approach all of my life like that. And I think that's what makes it rewarding. And that's also the thing that creates that tension. I didn't have the answer. I was working on it. I tried it a couple different ways and I still didn't get there, but I'm working on it and trying and pushing on things. And that applies to all of us. You're going to do that today a million times. And so the guy across the street from me that's working the gas pump, think about the different times that you've tried to optimize the way you clean the windows on your car. You've tried it 45 different ways over the years to find the ultimate way, but that's a creative exercise. So I don't think there's any difference. And I think it's important that we acknowledge that there's no difference. And any attempts to elevate these creative professions over anything else are probably doing a disservice to everybody.", "token_estimate": 692, "speakers": ["Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:48:59", "end_timestamp": "00:48:59", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "4bbbeb0a-b36c-43a4-a09c-7735c0954d5e", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:51:53): Beautiful. What is one of the biggest failures as a leader that you have experienced? Could be recent, could be earlier in your career?\n\nRyan Coulter (00:52:04): That's a great question. I think if you think about leadership as a two-way responsibility, there's only so much that you can do in this game of motivating and training. And then there's only so much that the other person can do too. And so I think sometimes, and this may not sound like a great failing of mine, but I can internalize failures as my own that really may have been failures within the other people and their ability to even know thyself to get down with what actually motivates them and put it on the table and I can beat myself up about it. I can't get this person. And we've had to let some people go fire some people. And you feel bad about that because you say, what did I do wrong? Why didn't we retain that person? And so anytime you lose an important person, you really start to internalize that and think, I did something wrong. Something is wrong here and something may well be wrong, but it may not be you. It may well be them. (00:53:14): And a lot of it may just honestly be not in their skills, but in their ability to know thyself and come to the table, what really motivates them, what they really want to be doing and sort how to get there. I'm trying to think of major failures and I can't think of some, but I'm sure if you ask my people I've worked with, they'll go through a laundry list. But I do think things that I did early on in my career that I speak out against now are micromanaging others. Just trying to forcing their hand to get to where you want them to be instead of letting them kind of go in the direction you want to go and having that be good enough. (00:53:55): I think that balancing between, and it's a really interesting line to try to tease out all the time in the world of making things. It's somewhere along the line between putting an idea on paper, doing a 3D model of it, building a prototype of it really, and then actually making a production thing happen. You lose the perfection in it. It's perfect. Only as an idea in your mind and every step towards reality, it actually gets a little less perfect, and it's probably in its least perfect form when it actually becomes a reality. But your job is to make actual things to put them in the market, to exchange them for money, for value with people. And so you have to balance your perfectionistic tendencies with reality. The difference between art and design is that design is there to make products that people actually buy. Where art can just be self-expression. (00:54:54): You can surely buy art, but art can be perfect because it really lives in the space of just that one object and not thousands of things that actually have to be injection molded or machined. So I think I'm saying that to say that there have been times in my career when I think I've probably held two tightly to an idea of something and driven it too hard maybe to the point of making it not even happen, where it's like where if I would've backed up, maybe that would've happened and people would've really found value in it. (00:55:33): So I think that's a thing that I've done and I've learned to back up and realize what is good enough and still maintain what I think everyone would say is a very high standard for myself and for the brand and for things that we do now. And I think everybody goes through this tendency, and I know that I've done it too, I'll just do it myself and you don't get it. Just step aside, let me do it. We're running out of time. And if there's almost one thing that I would tell and I do tell people not to do as they're learning to manage other folks, it's like, don't do that because two things happen. One, it's wildly demotivating, and again, your two jobs are to motivate and train. So you've just not done your job as you demotivate these people. Two, you didn't train them on how to do it or what they're supposed to do, so you also didn't do your job again. (00:56:29): And three, you're never going to get any value out of this investment that you've made in this person until you can get them there. And so understanding that hiring people and growing a team over the short term is wildly inefficient and expensive, and over the longer term, it's wildly valuable, but you have to understand the difference in the timeframe and the investment that it's going to take upfront. But the general assumption's always like, man, I'm going to go hire this person. That'll be easy hiring people, super easy, and they're going to show up and start doing this work I don't want to do, and that'll be great and my life will be great. And hiring people is very difficult. Getting them up to speed is difficult. Doing all this other stuff just takes a lot of time and engagement and real deal, like face-to-face investment and you don't know that until you've been through it.", "token_estimate": 1247, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:51:53", "end_timestamp": "00:52:04", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "fb08afa1-abf2-4a81-81e4-d6cf081ec3c0", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:57:20): I wish we had another hour to keep chatting, but I know that we don't. So I'm trying to prioritize the last question that I want to ask. What teams either in the outdoor industry or outside of the industry inspire you?\n\nRyan Coulter (00:57:36): Well, that's a really good question. I was very inspired by the team of people that I worked with at Burton during that period of time, and not just like the binding team, but Burton had done such an interesting job of pulling widely varied global talent from all over the world and getting them organized in this little town in Vermont of all places and having them work together to change the world. And I put a lot of this on Jake Burden himself. I had a lot of good, real interactions with Jake, and a lot of people did. I mean, Jake was, he's personable. He was there as a reference and a resource, and he let these kids, we were kids come in in their early twenties and he gave us millions of dollars of budget and said, make this thing, and was wise enough to know he wanted to influence it. (00:58:44): But kind of like we were talking about before, Jake didn't do anything but also did everything. Jake didn't have a desk in his office. He had a table. And this is a really important lesson, and it took me years to understand it. And I was like, how can Jake not have a desk? He had a table and couches, because Jake's job was to sit and talk to people, take info in, pass info out, motivate and train, train and motivate. So you don't do that sitting at a desk, looking out the window, you do that around a table, looking at people and talking, looking at images, looking at prototypes, talking about the stuff. And so the most magic stuff in that entire place happened around that table with Jake. And that was his job. He sat in that office all day with people going in and out of there as he shaped that. And so this team of people were in their twenties and they were enabled by Burton and Jake and were given the freedom to figure out what they could do. (00:59:43): That was amazing. I don't know that people get that opportunity very often. I am wildly grateful for that opportunity and it was such a confidence builder for me in that I don't think we effed it up too bad. We did a good job and there were a lot of us and we were all in that same boat. We were driven, we were smart people. We loved snowboarding. We came from all over the world and kind of met up there and we were maybe to a fault, one big family. There's really no line between work and life in that environment. But we did great stuff. And so many of those people have gone on to change the world in other ways. But I really think that was a special time. I remember one of the things I did at Burton, I was sort of the first women's binding, the Alexa was this awesome project where Burton at that point was just making, there were just bindings in different sizes and there were some real issues with that, particularly because the size curve for women's feet is very different than the size curve for men's feet. (01:00:41): So the men's small, medium and large just didn't fit any one in the women's realm very well. And so there was a very tactical problem that we could solve, but it meant sort of doubling the investment in snowboard bindings. Suddenly we're going to go from having one line to having two. And snowboard binding tools are giant and expensive. I mean, you're talking many hundreds of thousands of dollars. You got less than rights and three sizes of high box, and it's a big project. But we got Jake and the rest of the leadership team to buy off on this as an idea. And so we did it. And I remember walking up to present it at the sales meeting and you're on a stage and there's like 300 people and the lights are down. And as I'm walking up there, Jake grabs me by the arm and says, Hey, you better be right about this. (01:01:34): And he was like, not kidding. He's like, Hey, this needs to be right. We spent a ton of time and money and he was doing it kind of to rib me because sold him in on this and he's like, oh my gosh, we're going to write this check for millions of dollars to do this thing. Million. But I convinced him and he let us do that. He's like, man, you better be right about this. And I told him, I'm right. We're right. We did the right work here. And so he did that again to push me to kind of prod me, but wanted to see where I was. And I told him, and again, it's Jake Burton and I'm 23 working for his company. We got this, we're right. And I think that environment and that team, that was common, that kind of stuff was common. And that's just I think very rare. And still those folks, many of those folks are still people. I call my best friends and we're all in different places and things these days, but that was a very special time. And that group of people and what we were doing was impactful, I think, to the world and to the sport, but to each of us too in learning what we are capable of. I think that was the best time.", "token_estimate": 1261, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "00:57:20", "end_timestamp": "00:57:36", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "b621028e-a102-49bc-aa25-490fda7f1116", "episode_id": "c0f760fb-c058-44f0-8b5f-3d5bc156053d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a2", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:02:49): Is there anything that we haven't covered, Ryan, that you want to leave our audience with before we have to sign off for today?\n\nRyan Coulter (01:02:56): I mean, clearly I got lots to say, but No, I mean, I think we've covered the good stuff. I do think the most valuable things that I have to share are really about ways to help get the most out of other folks and your teams. And again, that has been the most rewarding part for me is watching other people actually come to their full fruition. That's super rewarding for me and rewarding enough that I've tried to invest a lot of my time in figuring out how to do that, how to get the most out of these people. Not for the company, but for themselves, for everybody. Everyone benefits when we do that. And so hopefully we've talked about that a lot and hopefully other people can contribute more to that cause because I do think it just makes the world a better place if we're getting the most out of each other and demand and expect the best out of each other.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:03:49): Yeah, well said. Well, if there's anything to leave our audience with, it's that we could all use stronger leadership in the world today. And Ryan, I appreciate you taking the time to share your perspective and lessons learned. This has been super interesting and I've definitely really enjoyed this conversation and have some things to think about for myself. So thank you.\n\nRyan Coulter (01:04:13): Yeah, cool. Thanks very much. I really appreciate you having me on here.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:04:17): Anytime. We could do a part two and I think we'd have another hour and a half to discuss easily.\n\nRyan Coulter (01:04:23): I'm always here for it.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:04:24): If folks want to follow along or connect with you or the brand, where can they go to find you?\n\nRyan Coulter (01:04:30): Best places are the James brand.com, kind of where everything lives and Instagram just at the James brand. Those are probably the best places. And then for more of my personal and professional stuff, LinkedIn, I'm always posting up articles and things there just as I come across 'em.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:04:46): Awesome. Beautiful. Well, Ryan, thank you again. I hope you have a good rest of your day and hopefully you can get up to Mount Hood soon once the snow starts to fall and get some turns in.\n\nRyan Coulter (01:04:55): It's happening now. We should have a foot this week. Amazing. Maybe this weekend.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:05:01): Alright, take care.\n\nRyan Coulter (01:05:02): Alright, thanks Cole. Bye. See you.", "token_estimate": 646, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ryan Coulter"], "start_timestamp": "01:02:49", "end_timestamp": "01:05:02", "podcast_index": 126, "episode_name": "EP: 126 | Leading a Creative Team | Ryan Coulter | The James Brand", "slug": "ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-125-leading-a-creative-team-ryan-coulter-the-james-brand", "guest_name": "Ryan Coulter", "guest_title": "Founder of The James Brand", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "‍Ryan and Cole talk about what it takes to lead a truly creative team. Listen to this one to jumpstart 2023 and your leadership skills!", "podcast_length": "1:05"}, {"id": "c478e054-79a5-4ec7-86f9-abb126368bd5", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "David Russo (00:00:00): If someone is missing a piece of communication or something isn't getting happened, if you can turn that accountability on yourself as the leader and say, okay, how can I change? How can I adjust to make the team successful? I think to me that's a much better approach.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:30): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hoborne. Today I'm sitting down with David Russe. He is the VP of sales and Marketing at Mammo North America. David, how are you?\n\nDavid Russo (00:00:40): I'm great, yeah, thanks for having me, Cole. Really looking forward to chatting today.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:45): Absolutely. Where are you calling in from?\n\nDavid Russo (00:00:48): Yeah, so I'm based here in Huntington, Vermont, just about half hour outside of Burlington, kind of nestled in the foothills here, close to the base camel sump. And really it's one of the, I guess, lesser known secrets of the state here. It's definitely like a recreational hotbed, but it's becoming increasingly popular with people like Ted King and others putting on big gravel races and stuff like that in the area. So it's a really cool place. More dirt roads than paved roads and tons of access to the outdoors.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:27): So I've never been to Vermont, but I have this romantic notion that if I go there, I'm going to find all of these amazing covered bridges leading across rivers, and the fall foliage is perfect. Am I wrong in believing that that's", "token_estimate": 374, "speakers": ["David Russo", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:00:00", "end_timestamp": "00:01:27", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "772d3766-a6f0-446c-b846-773afd5b2936", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "David Russo (00:01:45): Yeah, you're definitely not wrong. I mean, it's kind of funny. We're definitely frequently confused for being a part of Canada, or a lot of people have the notion that there's nothing here but cows. But yeah, I mean Vermont for me, it's a pretty special place. And growing up in New England, spending the majority of my life here, but having traveled quite a lot for work is definitely, in my opinion, it goes head to head with any of the outdoor recreation kind of hubs that you have out west with a very different feel in climate. And certainly the weather is not for everyone, but it definitely makes you appreciate those good days. And yeah, certainly we're famous for fall. We're famous for maple syrup, but we've also got a pretty big lake and it's a pretty fun time to be there in the summer as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:02:40): Well, we're recording this in early November, and it sounds like the snow is beginning to stick and you might be getting out up in the mountains and the hills here shortly. We've been trying to get this conversation on the books for, gosh, a couple months, few months now, but here we are, we're making it happen. And just to give folks an idea of what we're going to be diving into today, we're going to be talking all about leadership and the elements of good leadership, building a good team leading through change. And to kick it off, David, I would love if you could give us a quick introduction and a quick breakdown of your role with Mammo.", "token_estimate": 377, "speakers": ["David Russo", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:01:45", "end_timestamp": "00:02:40", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "ef2244b8-8492-4ba0-816e-4979bef9e3f3", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "David Russo (00:03:17): Yeah, so for me, I think my journey in the outdoor industry as a whole started working in retail, and I think that's really an experience that's relatable for a lot of people. That tends to be the foot in the door and kind of what opens your eyes to this actually being a career path. And for me, that started working for Eddie Bauer, working for just one of their small retail stores here in Burlington, Vermont. And then the neighbor down the street was the famous outdoor gear exchange, this absolute kind of community hub and focal point of the area. And my college roommate was working there at the time, and I really looked to all of those people there as like, wow, these are the really cool people who know everything. They're doing all the crazy adventures and I just want to be a part of that. (00:04:15): So yeah, after spending three years working for Eddie Bauer, I got a job working for Outdoor Gear Exchange and kind of leading the clothing departments there. So really getting to train staff on the sales floor, work with the buyers to bring in the right product and start interacting with brands and understanding how to teach and educate staff about product, how to come up with merchandising plans, how to best display and tell these brand stories. And for me, that was the first time that I really got to engage in that way with those brands. And that got me really excited about marketing and about the opportunities to tell stories. And for me in college I studied writing and I had the opportunity to write a little bit and had some stuff published in Rock and Ice and some other publications. And that was something for me that kind of led to moving into the e-comm space with outdoor gear exchange, doing some content writing and really growing out that skillset on the digital side of things and in marketing and getting to work with an agency and getting to understand and learn what SEO meant. (00:05:31): And that whole really launching off point of what we've been going through now, which is this kind of digital revolution, I would say in retail and how we approach retail. And that led me to the opportunity to go to mammo. I got hired to launch the website for us here in the US and really a part of bringing OO and North America to kind of the digital age. And so with that was able to grow my role and keep searching for new opportunities within oo, I got picked to be a part of what they were calling at the time, this business excellence program, which was sort of like a Six Sigma lean program, really focused on lean management tools and giving me a bit more of that formal skillset to be able to apply to my job, whether that was creating common understanding and mapping out processes, but also some of those kind of leadership skills as well. (00:06:39): So definitely feel like that was really a pretty pivotal thing for me to be able to grow into that skillset of now getting to work directly with essentially three directors for us in North America. So my team is made up of three female directors, Tiffany Brenna and Nicole. Nicole, I know Cole from a previous conversation. So those teams are essentially for us, a product team, a sales team, and then a marketing team. And really my goal is to be able to make sure that we're always tying everything together from a product marketing and sales standpoint and that those things are moving in the same direction. And I think a lot of people who work in this industry can probably tell you frustrations of those teams not being aligned or coming up with a really great idea, not having the product to support that idea and really that communication and making sure that everyone is kind of working together is my main goal in sort of a day to day.", "token_estimate": 942, "speakers": ["David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:03:17", "end_timestamp": "00:03:17", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "36ecd314-739d-4318-b1f7-7324954d0d84", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:07:50): Gotcha. When did you realize that you were a leader? I feel like you kind of gave us the expedited version of your career. You got hired originally at Mad Moot to work on the website and then tell us a little bit about that moment to now being the vp.\n\nDavid Russo (00:08:07): Yeah, I think for me, really what it came down to was I've always had this kind of curiosity and willingness and want or internal drive to do more to learn new things. My background at Outdoor Gear Exchange, I really got to work in almost every department in the store, and that was like every time I got to be in a position where I was doing the same thing and it was becoming too repetitive, I was like, alright, what else can I do? What else can I learn? Where are the opportunities? And that's always been that kind of internal driver for me. So I mean, early on at Mamo there were opportunities where back in 2018 we purchased this app Mountain Hub, and it was this really cool mapping software that you could map out different adventures, and there was think kind of Strava fat map style application. (00:09:10): And at the time when we purchased it, there was no one to run the app. It was like we were onboarding that team, it came in and it was sit down in the circle with the CEO and a few others and everyone's kind staring at each other like, Hey, who has the time to do this? And I had never run an app before. I had no experience in that, but I knew that Google has the answers to everything and that if I was willing to put myself out there that I could give it a shot. And so I think for me that attitude is what kept leading to these opportunities when everyone else was hesitating, I was willing to take that step forward and put myself out there, and it's a vulnerable position to be in because of course you can make mistakes and you will make mistakes, and that's all a part of it. (00:10:11): But I think for me, willingness to make decisions and willingness to be wrong is actually a separation for people in moving into a positions of leadership. And so that was really, for me, it was those increased opportunities of when everyone else was like, Hey, we don't know what to do. Who has a solution to come to the table, not only with a solution, but be willing to be the person to carry that extra weight and invest that time. And that's also I think something you have to ask yourself is if it's worth or if you want to invest that time, it doesn't come for free for sure. So there was definitely nights and weekends involved there in the beginning, and I think that that really was one of those examples that kind of set me up to continue to grow into this position. But yeah, it started off as well, it's small. (00:11:15): I would say the first team I was responsible for was two people. And then that opportunity grew into with again, just natural circumstances, people leaving the company positions changing us, needing to add new structures where every time the CEO here in North America kind turned to me and said, Hey, would you be willing to do this? My answer was yes, let's try it. Give me a shot at it. So yeah, that led to kind of this just organic growth of leading the direct to consumer part of our business to leading the B2B kind of wholesale part of the business to then taking over managing what we were doing for the demand planning, the buying, the merchandising, sort of the product tasks. And then later in 2021 also tying in and overseeing the marketing piece as well. So it really was that kind of one chunk at a time, one team at a time, and then trying to build out those team leaders and directors to directly support me because in the end as well, your team needs time. And I don't think any organization can be successful if one person has 30, 50, a hundred direct report twos. I don't know how you balance the one-on-one calls on the calendar that looks like that, so,", "token_estimate": 982, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:07:50", "end_timestamp": "00:08:07", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "50febf3e-1fd9-43a1-87f6-b58f04f5bc3f", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:12:52): Sure. Interesting. So that seems like you came into the company relatively in a few years. You kind of bounced around between all the different departments, started to understand the lay of the land and then found yourself in the position you are now. How do you think about your job as a leader?\n\nDavid Russo (00:13:09): Yeah, I think for me a lot of that comes down to setting, I would say setting the tone. And I think when you hear people say that phrase, some traditional examples of that is the CEO or the leader who shows up first in the office and they're there before everyone else and they leave last. And I think that's that classical setting the tone, if you will, or sort of establishing a culture of what work looks like. But for me, where that comes down to being most important is setting the tone when it comes to attitude and when it comes to energy. And for me, optimism is one of the most important attributes I think for myself in order to be successful in doing my job. And I really think a lot about how do I make sure I'm using energy for good? Because again, I think one of those separators and what I experienced in terms of maybe even going from being considered a manager to being in a leadership position is when you're a manager, you can afford to be talking with your team. (00:14:33): And when people are frustrated or someone's upset, you're kind of all just piling on. And yeah, that meeting was terrible and I can't believe it went that way, and I can't believe this person said that, but that's using that energy in a negative way and it's feeding into that. I think your responsibility as a leader, it really comes into always making sure that you're taking that one step back and saying, okay, how can I make sure that we can set that aside and keep moving in a positive direction and keep that momentum moving and kind of, yeah, really be, I wouldn't say in control, but sort of dictating the energy of the room and the energy of the team. Because I think more than anything, that energy in that momentum, moving in the right direction is what leads to success versus not. And it's sort of having a fast forgetfulness or sort of a very short memory of that happened, acknowledge that it happened, and how do we move on and how do we talk about a solution and moving in the right direction? That's really, for me, underlying all things for how I see my job and my most important responsibility is setting that tone leading with positive energy and always be sort of creating that momentum in a forward direction.", "token_estimate": 644, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:12:52", "end_timestamp": "00:13:09", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "94c3ea6b-52a5-4e91-9a8f-49e55a6a42ca", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:16:06): I love that you're channeling the energy of the room rather than telling people where they should or how they should be using that energy.\n\nDavid Russo (00:16:13): Yeah, and I think just another thing I would touch on there, Cole, is for me, there can be times where I think one of the best pieces of advice I ever got around energy, especially when you're in a room with people, whether that's giving a presentation, doing a training with staff in a meeting with a vendor or a client, et cetera, is acknowledge the distraction. And what I mean by that is if everyone's about to sit down and you, Cole are in the back of the room making a cup of coffee really quick and the spoon is clattering and there's this noise going on, instead of ignoring that and trying to start your conversation, acknowledge that distraction and maybe even make light of it and be like, oh, looks like Cole had a long night last night, needs some extra time to make a coffee. Let's give him a minute and then we'll get started. And that's another way that you're dictating that energy in the room and you're understanding that everyone's paying attention to that distraction. So instead of trying to hide from it, you acknowledge that distraction, you draw that energy, and then you kind of pull it back in and move it in the direction that you want it to go in.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:17:37): I can see that applying in other circumstances as well, not just in a very particular, in a meeting example, I could see that being applied on a much larger scale. Do you have any examples of acknowledging a distraction at a much higher level?", "token_estimate": 410, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:16:06", "end_timestamp": "00:17:37", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "be25027d-954a-412b-b0af-932ec62f61fa", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "David Russo (00:17:54): Yeah, I think another example of that might be, let's say for a business product shipping late. I think that's something where we've all experienced that during the past two years, supply chains were a mess. And those excuses about why something can't happen or why the team is upset about something or why something isn't going according to plan, to me, that's another time where you can acknowledge the distraction first and be like, look, I understand we're all disappointed by the fact that this product is late and that we had these plans. And you do have to create that acknowledgement and create that space that people's feelings around that are totally valid. But then you have to quickly create that opportunity to say, we understand this product is late, but we have 200,000 other products in our warehouse today. How do we get creative and come up with an opportunity to continue to move other product, continue to change our plan and do something different? So yeah, I think at scale that really can represent any opportunity or any time where there's disappointment, something doesn't go according to plan, quickly acknowledging that, letting people know that that's a valid concern, but then creating that opportunity for let's talk about a solution, let's talk about moving forward, I think, yeah, you see that happen every day.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:19:36): I love that we're talking about this now because I want to get into some more specific later in the episode. I want to get into some more specific scenarios or examples. But it sounds like as a whole, when you approach leadership directing energy and dictating energy is one of your key principles, I'm curious if there are any others that are kind of guiding beacons that you follow before we get into some of the weeds?", "token_estimate": 454, "speakers": ["David Russo", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:17:54", "end_timestamp": "00:19:36", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "eb0ee403-6c3a-4b0a-9e33-a4270b39f16a", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "David Russo (00:20:00): Yeah. For me, I would say in general, I think being empathetic is something you hear people talking a lot about. And to me that's really important as well. And I would say where empathy for me comes into play or how I would interpret empathy in a workplace environment is when something doesn't get done, your first reaction being, okay, why did that not happen? And understanding it from a personal level. So Cole, if you were on my team and we had talked about, I don't know, putting out a new social media campaign and the post never happened, and we talked about it being published on Tuesday and Tuesday comes and it didn't happen, instead of right away getting upset or coming to you and being like, Cole, what the hell? Why didn't this happen? It's Tuesday, this is the deadline. Why didn't you meet the deadline? (00:21:05): To me, leading with empathy comes into asking yourself, why might Cole not have been able to accomplish this understanding, Hey, maybe Cole has a lot going on at home right now, or, oh, his wife's traveling and he has to take care of the kids. Or Hey, when I look at the task list that I have for Cole, I asked him to do 10 other things that were due today, so maybe that's why it didn't happen. And then have that conversation and understand from your perspective why it didn't happen, and then how do you adjust, adapt to make sure that you can be successful in the future. But for me, that level of empathy I think is super important because I think you hear it all the time, it's like every leadership book right now is talking about empathy, but how do you apply that and what does that look like and what does that mean? (00:21:59): And so for me, that would be an example of applying that, but I think that is a super important piece. And then I would say other things like in general, accountability, transparency, kindness in a way, and ambition is a huge part of, I think being a leader and some of the leaders that I've worked with, oftentimes people struggle with the gap of where we are and where the ambition is and those kind of steps of how you get there sort of in a very classical business sense of current state versus target state, that path and creating a clear path and kind of guiding people along that path. That's the tactical day-to-day part of the job, but setting that target far enough and having that ambition. You hear people throw around the title of visionary oftentimes for people, and I think there's a clear reason why, but I think having that ambition to me is a really important aspect. (00:23:15): But the counterbalance to ambition is always patience. And I think you need that as well, and you need that long-term view. And you need, for me, the way I evaluate my career right now, for example, I'm thinking I have 30 years left minimum of working. So whenever I'm upset about a decision in a one month timeline or three month timeline or a six month timeline, I'm like, I've got 30 year runway, I've got a lot of time to grow, to make mistakes, to improve. So how do I set myself up for the best next 30 years and not just be hyperfocused on the now", "token_estimate": 787, "speakers": ["David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:20:00", "end_timestamp": "00:20:00", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "ba481134-adbc-4aa6-88dc-f8ee8a96dc98", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:23:58): I want to back up a little bit. So you were talking about empathy and approaching challenges or things that didn't happen or misunderstandings with the team. From a state of empathy, how do you balance that with accountability? And in the example that you gave where maybe I didn't do the thing I was supposed to do, how do those balance, because I could see them, I mean they're, I guess I see them as balancing acts.\n\nDavid Russo (00:24:24): Yeah. Yeah. I would say for me, accountability is something that I always hold first for myself, then second for the team, and oftentimes I would even say maybe a critique or weakness of mine would be I like to take ultimate accountability or almost in some ways a radical approach to accountability for myself, where accountability in a lot of ways helps with being able to improve something. If I put all the accountability on you, Cole, for example, I only can improve so much your ability to do something, a lot of that is going to lie with you, but if I take accountability for you not getting something done, it puts the kind of control back in my place. So just to kind of put an example to it, something that I see happen a lot, especially with companies going remote, all of us working with these new tools, organizing your team, organizing information is super challenging. (00:25:36): When we sat down to play in this conversation, you're like, oh, pick a time through the Calendly link and there's now a tool and a digital tool and an online tool for literally everything. And with that, people frequently can't find stuff, they miss an invite, it goes to the wrong place, et cetera. And those are oftentimes the quick easy knee-jerk frustration reaction would be, Cole, why did you miss the meeting invite? Why did this happen? And for me, my approach to the accountability of that is, if you didn't get the invite, that's on me and I take accountability for that. How do I understand for the team the most successful way to communicate? And that could be different for different people. You might have someone who is digital native, they use a hundred different apps, they're super comfortable with it, you roll out a new tool, they get it within the first 10 minutes and they don't lose the invite. (00:26:43): But maybe you have someone on your team who's 50 and you just move them to being a remote workforce, they've never had a laptop before. And then sending that person an email or maybe even calling them before a meeting to remind them that the meeting is happening is the personalized approach that you need to take. And so for me, it's much more about if someone is missing a piece of communication or something isn't getting happened, if you can turn that accountability on yourself as the leader and say, okay, how can I change? How can I adjust to make the team successful? I think to me, that's a much better approach. But going back to that balancing act with being empathetic towards the team, but also being able to hold them accountable, I think clear expectations around when something isn't going to get done, how do you communicate it? (00:27:41): If you're going to miss a deadline, how do you come back and communicate that and how do we have enough heads up where it's not 10 minutes before the post is supposed to go live and you say, Hey, I had a really busy day. I'm sorry I'm not going to be able to get to this, but if we have a couple check-ins during the week, if a day out before that post, you're sending me a message on Slack or you're giving me a quick phone call or you're sending me an email, Hey, I don't see this happening, I don't think I'm going to be able to meet this timeline, how do we adjust? That to me is that balance of accountability versus being empathetic and understanding the situation.", "token_estimate": 957, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:23:58", "end_timestamp": "00:24:24", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "08adea8c-b649-41bf-982d-69498d3c2ef5", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:28:26): Sure. So let's transition a little bit and talk about a building a team and nurturing a team. And I know that your role with Mammo and just some of the changes at Mammo over the last few years has greatly impacted how you approach this challenge or this task. Could you give us the quick history of what you learned from some of the changes that you've been around and how that affects how you look forward to building a future team or building a team in the future?", "token_estimate": 123, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:28:26", "end_timestamp": "00:28:26", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "196b3401-77f6-45e3-82e2-a2f80395f440", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "David Russo (00:29:01): Yeah, so I would say for me, one of the biggest learnings, and really that was at mah mood, and that wasn't something that I don't think a book can teach you or I don't think you're going to learn in a college class, or maybe not even a mentor can tell you, but what I saw play out for us, and maybe this is a very specific example to our organization, but when there's a change in leadership at the highest level, so let's say CEO level change for a company, what people don't realize is that the history and in the kind of hierarchy of who are the top performers at the company, who are the people with the most responsibility or even who are the favorites of the leadership team, when you bring in a new CEO, everyone goes back down to zero. Everyone is at an even playing field. (00:30:04): And what you see very quickly is that showing that you have a positive attitude in that environment, showing a willingness to take on responsibility, showing a willingness to provide value or make decisions or create calm and certainty for the organization. Those are the really valuable skills during a time of change. And anytime you have that really high level change, it's very hard for people to adapt because you can have, whether that's like a C-suite or a team of directors or a team of senior managers who are making the decisions for the company, a new leadership's going to have a completely new philosophy over how to make decisions and how to lead the organization. And so those people who are used to sitting in the closed boardroom environment and planning out a strategy overnight, they might not be invited to that table anymore. And so for everyone, it very quickly becomes this place where you have to forget the past and bring that really positive optimistic mindset. (00:31:23): And so one of the ways that you look at building a team coming out of that environment is who are those early adopters to that change or that change in strategy? Who's coming with that positive attitude, who can create a sense of security and kind of really in that classical sense of like, Hey, follow me, we're going in this direction. Who can be those kind of trendsetters within the organization? And I think identifying those people is a really great way to lay a foundation for a new strategy or for a new change or for a new organizational structure and then create teams behind those people. But what's really interesting to see is if you go through multiple of those changes within a three year or five year, et cetera timeframe, that reset backed to zero in my experience, happens every time. And so even if, I don't know, let's say you were in a management position and you had one direct report, two, and you got a full new leadership change, they're evaluating the entire organization and maybe they say, Hey, we really love Kohler. (00:32:45): We really love this other person and the attitude and what they're bringing, and we think they have a great vision, you could get put into a new position leading five people almost right away, because those are those times where you really have to understand that timing aspect of this is a time for change, and everyone here is looking for who is going to be reliable, who is coming with the right attitude. And so it's a very much a time for opportunity for people within the organization. And so I think that to me is something that I saw happen a few times that I realized or understood the scope of what was happening. And then when put in a position of leadership, looked to who are the other people within the organization who are also understanding this and who also want to come with a solution oriented mindset and who want to bring that positive energy. (00:33:50): And those are really the people that traditionally, it's not performance based, it's not like this person doing the best job, it's capable, but attitude is everything. And I think that that's such an understated thing in business where everyone wants to check a box, everyone wants to get a certain degree, everyone wants to have a certain amount of experience. We all approach jobs through a very traditional job description lens, but those kind of soft skill moments are what's missing for a lot of people, and that they don't realize that attitude trumps a lot of those other things. And a willingness to learn and a willingness to put yourself out there. And don't get me wrong, I mean there's a lot to be said for experience, and I think experience can set you up to be most successful or to be one of those people that is then leaned on in that kind of new environment. But for me, just without meeting someone, if I just looked at two resumes, one had experience, but one clearly was communicating more of a passion, a willingness to learn the appropriate attitude, et cetera, et cetera, that person to me is a more interesting person to interview than just someone who on paper has the experience that you're looking for.", "token_estimate": 1246, "speakers": ["David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:29:01", "end_timestamp": "00:29:01", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "7bee4694-75aa-4e18-8de9-9689609ddef5", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:35:24): Does that, I feel like that reflects the, gosh, there's some saying experience skills can be taught, but attitude is much harder to teach something that effect.\n\nDavid Russo (00:35:38): Yeah, no, I think for sure, like you said, you're not going to be able to change someone's personality or their outlook as much as you can. You can anyone today who feels completely lost using Microsoft Excel after three YouTube tutorials and maybe a four week class, they can all of a sudden look like an Excel master. But that's not going to happen with someone who by default has a negative energy or is always looking to place blame on someone else or is unwilling to collaborate with a team, et cetera. Those negative energy moments, that's much more detrimental than not having a skill.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:36:32): So when you're interviewing someone or you're considering someone for a team position, how much of your decision making is based on just the gut feeling of a connection with someone? Would you prefer to sit through three lunches with someone to get to know them versus looking at their portfolio or their resume or whatever they've been able to produce?", "token_estimate": 297, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:35:24", "end_timestamp": "00:36:32", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "2f5ac645-7e25-4764-b521-add92c0e6fb4", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "David Russo (00:36:57): Yeah, I would say for me, there's always going to be that very basic expectation of, okay, for this type of role, we want to see someone with five years of experience or seven years of experience or 10 years of experience. And I think in some ways, a lot of that is actually more of a test of maturity or a test of professionalism than it is that just being somewhere for five years means you have gained a certain skillset. Because to me, just time alone in and of itself isn't necessarily a good measurement. But I think a lot of the reason for requiring seven years of experience, for example, is to get someone with a certain level of maturity or professionalism in a workplace environment. Basic things like emailing people respectfully, returning phone calls, showing up to work on time, being dressed appropriately for the meeting, which in the outdoor industry is actually kind of a funny thing to balance because professionalism looks very different in our industry. (00:38:09): And if we're meeting a client or a customer or a vendor to go skiing for the day, then you should dress that you're going skiing for the day versus being in the office and kind of the expectation just being, Hey, show up in clean clothes and look presentable enough. I am not expecting anyone to be in a suit. But yeah, I think so for a resume, yeah, there's a certain aspect of testing or litmus testing, that kind of professionalism piece. But then after that I want to see a level of understanding, does this person know what it takes to perform the job? Do they seem like they get the ask? And that to me is more important than necessarily the hard skill behind it, if that makes sense. If I were to hire for a dealer service position, does that person know what dealer services entails? Do they have a general understanding of the expectations for that job? But then the difference between someone who has great communication skills, who's friendly and enjoys talking to people versus someone who previously spent three years as a dealer service rep at a different job, but they actually don't like talking to people. (00:39:45): Those two things, it's like I see that quite often where people are in positions, but it's actually not necessarily aligned with what their strengths are or what they enjoy doing. So yeah, I think to your point, the interview process for me is the most helpful time to get to know a candidate and start to be able to recruit and build a team. And that is a lot more about just fit feeling attitude and kind of sussing out also, does this person want to get their hands dirty or get into the weeds, get in the trenches, whatever cliche you want to use, or are they someone who's looking to sit back and make big picture decisions and not necessarily do the work? And then understanding the size of your organization and what you need. Because in a small organization, you need the doers. And that's another aspect for myself and for Chris, our CEO, is we're not taking tasks and saying, oh, that's not my job, or that's below me. (00:40:59): When we're at a trade show, we're right alongside people building that trade show booth. When we have a customer meeting, I'm going to Whole Foods beforehand and making sure that we have seltzer water and fruit and things for people for the meeting. I'm not tasking that out to assistant. And so that's just an aspect of our organization, but it's also for me, the leaders in my career that I've respected the most and jelled with the most are also the people who are willing to do the work. I love in movies when you see the restaurant owner and he is out front sweeping the steps and he is taking pride in his space and he's just as involved as the rest of the team. I think that's, to me, that is a very important aspect of building a team and having that culture.", "token_estimate": 964, "speakers": ["David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:36:57", "end_timestamp": "00:36:57", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "74e1e403-b2fb-434a-9c0a-b32a1fd19692", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:41:59): How do you know when you've built a team that's working well, are there outputs or indicators that you can measure when it's all working?\n\nDavid Russo (00:42:11): Yeah, I think certainly there's the traditional way of establishing KPIs, key performance indicators around we want to see growth of X percentage in revenue, or we want to see engagement on social media at this percentage, or we want to see an increase of followers or whatever metric you want to measure. I think there's different aspects of, and those are important and valuable. Setting targets and setting clear goals I think is a really important aspect of creating success not only for you but for the team as a whole. Some of the analogies I use for my team around why setting goals are important is if you were to go to a firing range and there's no target at the end of where you're shooting, you'll still probably have fun and you can do that for a while, but at a certain point you're actually going to get bored because you don't know, am I getting better? Am I getting more accurate? Am I hitting the bullseye or not? And you're just eventually just going to stop shooting, you're going to stop doing what you're doing because you have no way of measuring that. (00:43:33): And I think that, I don't know, let's say another example of that would be you're, I don't know, you're heavyweight boxer and you're aiming for a certain weight to be able to get into that weight class without a scale, it becomes extremely difficult to know if you gained enough weight to be able to compete and fight in that heavyweight weight class. And so you do need some of those metrics or some of those goal measurements to know, okay, I feel like the team is working well, but also the team has that buy-in. The team has something that they're working towards and that they feel good about that. And I think those goals have to be beyond just revenue targets. They've got to be specific to a team, they've got to be specific to tasks in order for people to really feel a part of something and bought in. (00:44:30): And then on the flip side, purely just a leadership perspective, I would say one of the most ironic aspects of leadership is that when the team is doing their best, they are going to get all the credit. And when they are not doing well, you as the leader are going to be taking all the blame. And that's something that you have to be comfortable with and you have to understand that that's an aspect of the job. And the other side of when the team is going really well, you often feel like you aren't needed. And that's a really tough thing I think for new leaders that doesn't get talked about because every leadership book I've ever seen or read tends to come from someone who's been a leader for 20 years. And so they maybe don't even remember what it was like when they first took on that responsibility or they first started leading a team. (00:45:30): But when you go from being someone who's a doer or executing everything on your own to being someone who's teaching and coaching and having other people actually executing the work, when all of that's going really well, that team doesn't need your feedback. They don't need you to teach them something. They don't need a second set of eyes on things before they send the email. They don't need you to take a look at a marketing brief before sending it to a partner. And that can be really challenging for the leader to understand that in a way you're going to feel unneeded or almost unwanted. And so you have to start to really fuel your personal battery on the success of the team. And then look long-term in those moments where you have that stability and you have that calm, then how are you creating that ambition? (00:46:36): How are you setting the further target? How are you looking forward thinking? Because I would say for me, the majority of the time, going almost back to one of your first questions, what does your day to day look like? What do you do? What I should have said is, all I do is solve problems, because that's the other aspect of leadership is you're going to spend the majority of your time solving problems. And that's another thing that when you first step into that role, you're like, man, people only come to me when something's broken. And that's challenging to deal with and to digest and to finish your day feeling like you've accomplished something because all you've done all day is deal with the broken things. But you start to learn how to really get engaged with and enjoy the problem solving and enjoy coming up with solutions. And it's just another aspect, again, where it's all about that energy of do you dwell on the fact that things are broken and feel negative, or do you feel positive when you solve something and you create that momentum and you're continuously improving something?", "token_estimate": 1223, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:41:59", "end_timestamp": "00:42:11", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "647ae895-6528-4d83-a600-9245af2a3b1f", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:47:59): How do you balance your own personal feeling of feeling of contentment or your own ego or your own, I'm struggling to find the word, but if the team is thriving and you aren't quote unquote needed, how do you make sure that you still show up in a good place?\n\nDavid Russo (00:48:17): Yeah, I think there, there's definitely humility would be another buzzword or topic in leadership for sure. And I think for me, where that comes into play is exactly in those moments of also understanding that creating an outlook of continuous improvement and kind of that continuous improvement as a phrase for me started in that Lean Six Sigma environment where they talk about process improvement. And it's very much in an operational sense. It's like, okay, here's all the steps to a process. Identify the constraint in the process. Where's the bottleneck? How do we improve this process? Okay, now we're more efficient. But that same mindset of continuous improvement can be applied to every aspect, your personal life. How do you improve motivating your team? How do you improve running more efficient meetings? How do you improve your email response time, your time management, your health work-life balance? There's so many different pieces that you can go after. And so I would say in those moments of feeling like, Hey, the team's doing great and they don't need me, how do you take a step back and go for one of those continuous improvement projects? And that could be any multitude of things, but I think that to me is the best approach there. (00:50:10): And then also understanding that there are moments or times where it also might be time to walk away or time to look for the next challenge or time to take on more or different responsibilities. And I also came from a place in my career where I wasn't someone who went to business school, got an MBA, and immediately was given a management or director level role in a company and then only ever worked as a leader outside of my outdoor industry experience. I spent time working in retail for staples. I spent time as a limousine driver. I spent time as a counselor for an afterschool program, as a summer camp counselor at a soccer camp, at a video rental store. I've worked so many different jobs, and what I think that experience gained me is a different level of empathy towards individuals in positions in an organization where you understand when you're in a position and it seems like there's no opportunity for you to grow, how demoralizing that is. And so for me, I'm also always looking at where are there opportunities that I can step aside and give someone an opportunity to grow into something new? How can I structure teams? How can I structure responsibilities that always guarantees or kind of fosters an environment for people to be able to grow Because the last thing I want to do is create a situation where someone just feels like they're up against the hard ceiling and they're like, man, until my boss retires, I'm never going to be able to do anything else. (00:52:11): So yeah, I think that's also in those moments of, Hey, everything's going really well. The team feels like it's in a good place here. That's another sort of self-reflective time of can someone else step into an elevated role? Can someone else move and can you create opportunity for other people? So yeah, I think that's another key piece of it. And don't get me wrong, I think maybe I'm painting the wrong picture that I have tons of moments where I don't feel needed. And it would be amazing if my job felt like that every day. I'd feel really good about how I was doing. But those are, yeah, you get those moments and you get plenty of other moments where it feels like nothing's going right. So there's always a balance there.", "token_estimate": 950, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:47:59", "end_timestamp": "00:48:17", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "e9ae90f3-3a0a-4d15-97b0-40b3a69c4c4d", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:53:03): When we connected previously before this call, you had mentioned this idea one year of learning, one year of doing one year of teaching. And I'm curious if you can break down that three-step process and how does that contribute to the overall development and growth of a team member and ultimately helping launch them into their own career or their own direction?\n\nDavid Russo (00:53:27): Yeah, I think for me, what I saw happen time and time again is, and I would hate to have people listen to this who I've worked with and be too, but I think it's really easy for people to get impatient. And that patient's piece is really hard to grasp in that big, big scale. And when someone starts to have success or when someone starts to gain free time in their job or they start to get comfortable with their workload, the ambitious people are always going to be like, I want more. I want more. How do I do more? How do I take on more? And for some people, it's very much like, I want to be a manager. I want to manage people. I want to have a team. How do I do this? And so when I saw trying to be able to kind of foresee that is to think about development plans or think about setting people up for, Hey, let's view this on a three year timeframe, or let's view this on a two year timeframe and let's talk about what your goals are and let's talk about a future salary and let's talk about these different things, and then let's set up these kind of benchmarks for these different milestones that you can hit on this path towards that ultimate goal. (00:54:54): And I think what that accomplishes is it really create a sense of ownership for that person of if I can follow these steps, then I can achieve these goals, then I can get this increase in my salary, this increase in responsibilities, I can gain these things. And so in kind of building out that framework, that's where I kind of saw this pattern of really what it comes down to for those team members is it takes most people a full year of really learning something before you get a grasp of it. And the outdoor industry, we're very seasonal in our approach, and everyone knows that there's sell-in season for the sales team for spring summer and for fall winter. And there's go to market plan for spring, summer and for fall winter. But oftentimes those products are actually different in spring summer and in fall winter. (00:55:53): And maybe those retailers you work with are different in spring summer than they are in fall winter. And so even in that one year of doing or that one year of learning that first year, you're actually never getting to repeat a process because it's a unique process in spring, and then it's a unique process and fall. And so you're not actually applying the learning until the following time through. And that's hard for people to grasp as well, is we have these seasonal timelines for my buying team, for the demand planning team, it's like they place three intercompany orders and then they have a big break, and then they place three intercompany orders, and then they get back to another spring and they get to see in that following year, were we write, did we make a mistake? Did we buy too much? What's happening? And so you really need those longer timeframes to be able to actually start to make improvements. (00:56:53): And so yeah, that's where I really saw this one year of learning being, okay, how do we just understand the timeline? How do we understand the steps of the process? How do I understand who I need to talk to, who I am going to get resources from? Who are my collaborators? And also what's the impact of my decision making? And you see that in that first year or so often where someone makes a decision, doesn't understand the impact of that decision, and then someone else on the team gets upset or gets hurt by that process, and then everyone learns, and then hopefully in the next year you improve on that. So yeah, so you have that year of learning, and then the second year is really that year of doing, or oftentimes it can be a year of improving. And the doing thing is about, that's the moment where you hand over the trust where you say, look, you've got ownership over this. (00:57:53): We've been doing this together for the last year. I feel super confident in you. And communicating confidence is another thing that's like, that's the supercharger for an employee on your team. The time that I'm most successful as a leader is when our CEO says, David, I trust you in whatever decision you make. I'm behind you a hundred percent. And then I feel like I'm a superhero. I feel like I can do anything. And so communicating that confidence to your team is super important and super valuable. And so that second year, it's all about communicating that confidence, creating that sense of ownership, kind of setting them free and allowing them to start to implement some of their own ideas into the process, into creating improvements and really getting to do their job for that full timeframe. And then in that third year, teaching creates an opportunity for you to learn in a different way. (00:59:04): And oftentimes what I've found through teaching, through presenting, through doing trainings is that input, that feedback that you're getting is the fine tuning and the adjustment and the sort of like, wow, I didn't realize that I need to change the way I talk about this, or people don't understand when I say it this way. And so teaching really gives you that perspective to improve in a way that just doing it over and over and over again doesn't give you. So I think it's really important to create opportunities for people to teach, and whether that's just creating a better common understanding for the whole team, which is the social media team might be just teaching or presenting what they do as their job. Because a huge part of creating a successful culture is not having animosity about, oh, the marketing team, they just goof off all the time and film videos and they don't even work. (01:00:04): Or all the sales team does is fly on airplanes and go out to dinner and they don't work. And then the warehouse team is like, we're in here every day slaving away, and the finance team is like, all you guys do is send us invoices and we have all the unfunded work. So teaching is also not just about handing that task off or getting someone else to learn it, it's also teaching the whole organization what do we do? What do the different teams do, how to create this common understanding, how do we break down the kind of silos and just make people feel like they're a part of that bigger team? So yeah, I think those kind of three things to me, our great way to approach whether that's a very practical building, a three-year plan, approaching it from an employee development standpoint, thinking about a salary target, and giving someone those clear goals so that they feel ownership, they feel like they're a part of the process, they're engaged, versus just also the reality of some of those tasks that you need time in order to do things. And obviously there's always a balance there. The finance team is probably going to tell you that if you're the accounts payable person after two months of paying regular invoices, you've probably learned the steps and what to do. So maybe that timeline is faster, but I think in at least my departments, everything is so tied to the season. And because our industry works in these big two blocks of the seasons, that's where I've really seen this kind of three year lens make sense.", "token_estimate": 1899, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "00:53:03", "end_timestamp": "00:53:27", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "92312175-e0ff-4aa0-a86c-808cd36b0c0f", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:02:02): Yeah. Well, David, I feel like we've just kind of cherry picked a few ideas throughout this conversation, and I wish that we had more time to continue cherry picking ideas. We've got a little bit of time left. Is there anything else that you want to wrap up our conversation with? Anything else that you want to leave with our audience?\n\nDavid Russo (01:02:23): I think for me, one of the things I would say or maybe to own up to a mistake I've made or a learning that I would pass on is understand the impact of making time for your team and know what happens when you aren't able to make that time. And that can be being double booked for a meeting or in saying, Hey, I'm so sorry. I'll be there in a half hour. We're 15 minutes late. Or it could be you're in a meeting and you get a phone call from a customer or from a person on the leadership team or from someone else and saying, Hey, I'm super sorry. I need to step out and I need to take this call. Those are another aspect of maybe that energy topic where what you're not realizing in the moment is that you're actually introducing some of that negative energy and you're creating this disappointment or you're creating this sense of your team maybe feels like you have more important things to do or you're too good for them. (01:03:34): And learning from my wife and just listening to her work from home is just small things. If her supervisor has to reschedule their one-on-one because she's too busy, and even just pushing it back a day, hearing that disappointment of like, oh, it feels like she doesn't have time for me, that is something that I've realized and been able to internalize and say, Ooh, yeah, I think I've probably done that to my team several times. And you don't understand the impact of those things until you get to be the one on the outside listening and kind of hearing from my wife and understanding like, oh, wow, yeah, I've been that person and I've kicked someone's two days down the road or canceled it for the week and said, Hey, we'll just catch up next week. And understanding that your time is probably the most valuable resource for your team and also the biggest way for you to communicate that you care about them. And so that's something that I would say I've caught myself doing and I'm looking for opportunities to improve on. And I think for all leaders, understanding that and creating that balance of you're going to have to manage your time and in a very financial, literal way. Your time might be more valuable than other people's time, but you have to understand the impact of giving time and taking time away. And when you take time away, there is a big negative impact to that", "token_estimate": 678, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "01:02:02", "end_timestamp": "01:02:23", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "0fc7fa60-325c-46b6-8043-df8ac7ae34d1", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:05:28): To send us home. Are there any books, are there any leaders that you look up to that you go to for inspiration or ideas? Are there any resources that you would recommend aspiring leaders or leaders who are listening to check out if they want to learn more?\n\nDavid Russo (01:05:47): Yeah, I think probably just in listening to some of the things I brought up, people can tell. I'm definitely a pretty big fan of Brene Brown and just her topics on being brave and being vulnerable and really, I would say any of her books, her TED Talks, I think she has a lot of great resources on leadership. Also, we mentioned it before Cole and we chatted, but I'm definitely a big Gary Vaynerchuk fan, and I think he's really interesting and definitely someone who's like, he can be polarizing and how he communicates. I think a lot of people give him negative feedback on him swearing all the time and him being kind of always this super high energy. But I just personally, I really relate to a lot of the things that he says and I kind of really enjoy. I know he uses humor in a very nuanced way that I really appreciate. (01:06:55): I think one of my favorite super short interviews he did, this guy came up to him at the conference that he put on and he was like, Hey, Gary, I really want to get more into public speaking and I want to put myself out there more and all these things. Is there a resource where I can find speaking opportunities, keynote engagements, everything. And Gary kind of looks at him and is like, oh, there's this really great website. It's perfect for what you're asking for. And the guy's like, okay. He goes, take a note. And he is like, it's called Google, and he is just Google. It has all the answers. There's no secrets here. And so he almost, in a way, has this kind of, yeah, no bullshit kind of attitude. Just do it. You can do it. And for some people they kind of categorize that as the negative aspects of this grind culture and other things. (01:07:48): And I think with all people, you can pick apart good and bad. But yeah, for me, I found a lot of value in a lot of his leadership things. And I think similar to Brene Brown, he's pushing a lot more of the soft skill approach side of things. Sort of like the emotional intelligence side of things, EQ over IQ and really just being compassionate, being transparent, being empathetic. I think those are huge things. And then recently I was even just listening to his interview with the founder of Kind the snack company, and I really, really liked the snippet that they shared about the difference between being kind and being nice and that being kind, or I guess first to frame it being nice is if you see that someone has something stuck in their teeth, you just kind of don't say anything because you don't want to embarrass them. (01:08:52): And that's being very nice about it being kind, is actually tapping that person on the shoulder and saying, Hey, I just wanted to let you know that you have something stuck in your teeth and you might want to go to the bathroom, take that out. And that might be really embarrassing for that person. They might not know you. It might be uncomfortable for you to share that, but that's the kind thing to do. And I think that people misinterpret kindness and the place that it has in business. But I think that that's a great example of also just kind of saying what you need to say and delivering that message even when it's a hard message to deliver and that's an important thing to be able to bring to the table. So yeah, those are a few of the things. I mean, Simon Sinek is another one where I really like Leaders Eat Last and some of the resources that he has. So those are the three for me that I'd say I've been most interested in recently. Yeah.", "token_estimate": 951, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "01:05:28", "end_timestamp": "01:05:47", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "514ec173-fec6-41f5-a369-e18c73d73a02", "episode_id": "b3f5c5d0-595b-4d60-b6b3-c3445178b54a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a4", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:09:59): Okay. Awesome. Awesome. Well David, I appreciate you taking the time. Speaking of time to share your thoughts and some of the things that you've been learning along the way. Like I said, I feel like we've just scratched the surface with some of these ideas, but if folks are interested in connecting with you and maybe diving deeper, where can they go to find you or connect with the company?\n\nDavid Russo (01:10:26): Yeah, from a company standpoint, it's Mammo. Na is the at Mammo NA is the Instagram tag feel.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:10:35): Just Google\n\nDavid Russo (01:10:36): It. Yeah, feel free to drop us a DM there and we're happy to respond and chat with people.com. Is the website pretty easy? M-A-M-M-U-T? We do have kind of a funny brand name to pronounce. And then, yeah, for me personally, I'd be happy to share my personal email, Instagram handle, and you can drop it in the show notes for folks to reach out. I'm an open book and I definitely really love connecting with other people in the industry, sharing ideas, getting to chat with folks. That's something I see a of value in and creating opportunities to be a mentor and to be mentored. I think that's huge and that's super important. And I definitely think that that's, yeah, I'm always looking for people to connect with, to learn from, so.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:11:30): Awesome. Alright, David, well thank you. Thank you for all the work that you're doing in inspiring leaders in the outdoor industry, and I hope you enjoy the process of continuing and continuing to learn and grow. And maybe let's check back in and maybe we'll do a part two and find some more ideas to Cherry Pick.\n\nDavid Russo (01:11:49): Awesome. Yeah, thanks Cole. It was great chatting with you today.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:11:51): Alright, take care.", "token_estimate": 454, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "David Russo"], "start_timestamp": "01:09:59", "end_timestamp": "01:11:51", "podcast_index": 127, "episode_name": "EP: 127 How to Build a Team | David Russo | Mammut", "slug": "ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-127-how-to-build-a-team-david-russo-mammut", "guest_name": "David Russo", "guest_title": "VP of Sales and Marketing", "categories": ["leadership-and-people"], "short_description": "David shares his perspective on how to build a team, what it takes to be a good leader, the importance of emotional intelligence (EQ) over IQ, and asking questions and being curious.", "podcast_length": "1:12"}, {"id": "20b98fca-f879-472a-b361-1baf25ec6639", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:01): Are brands wasting money on social?\n\nSam Van Boxtel (00:04): I just see so many brands posting every day, but what's the point of it and why? So I just see it across the board so many different ways of brands, either not giving organic, social the respect and wasting money by missing out on stuff, but just as often putting too much respect and wasting all the manpower and capital that you're spending on that 20-year-old who's doing your social.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:31): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. I'm your host, Cole Heer. On this podcast, you'll hear from leaders in the outdoor marketing industry discuss the gritty details of their work, as well as the latest challenges and lessons they're learning along the way. If you want to hone your craft and become a stronger marketer, then this podcast is for you. This podcast is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Heel, born. Today I'm sitting down with Sam Van Boxell. He is the founder of Climbers. Craig. Sam, how are you?\n\nSam Van Boxtel (01:07): I'm doing great. Thanks for having me on the podcast.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:10): Well, Sam, I'd love if you could give us just a quick introduction to yourself, where you're calling in from and what the climbers CRA is.\n\nSam Van Boxtel (01:17): Yep. So calling in from Portland, Oregon. Yeah, climbers CRA is a agency that I started with Tom Randall. You might know him from Lattice training Wide boys, many of his climbing businesses, but we founded it as the climbing industry, social media agency. We want to help brands grow on social media. We just feel like the climate industry was kind of behind in some ways on social.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:42): So the topic we're going to be diving in today is are brands wasting money on social? And there's a bunch of different ways to look at this, but before we dive into some of the nitty gritty, I'm curious, why is this a topic that you're passionate about? Is this something that you run across frequently? Kind of set the stage for us before we dive in.", "token_estimate": 543, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "00:01", "end_timestamp": "01:42", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "95d77c71-d182-4f29-b2d7-3d32f836fb90", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (02:04): Yeah, I thought it was interesting to hear from an agency's point of view. I almost want to revert, I just see so many brands, and you'll listen to this podcast, you might be like, yes, we need to do organic. And I almost want to say, Hey, hey, hold up, hold up, hold up. If you're a climbing gym, if you're certain things you probably shouldn't be. And I kind of want to touch on some of the sides of, I did another podcast pretty recently and I had a bunch of outreach after that and they were like, oh, so you said do this. And then I said, well, okay. So that was general. That was general advice. That was advice to this case. You're actually in this other boat and that would be a waste to put this much effort in. So I just see across the board so many different ways of brands, either not giving organic social respect and wasting money by missing out on stuff, but just as often putting too much respect and wasting all the manpower and capital that you're spending on that 20-year-old who's doing your social. (03:14): And it's like, yeah, you're posting every day, but what's the point of it and why? Or the third category, what we used to do a long time ago is maybe you blow up really big. We had one video hit 250 million views. No joke, it's on climbers Craig. If you look, it was the best few days ever until you realize you got a bunch of terrible followers who don't care about you and just join the boat off one video. So maybe you go too big and now you're wasting your money because you got too big with the wrong people. So I think there's a lot of ways we can hit this topic. I just want it to be not the standard, how to grow on social. I wanted to be avoid these pitfalls because that's almost more impressive than growing really fast.", "token_estimate": 446, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "02:04", "end_timestamp": "02:04", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "4bef1050-0b0a-4714-a583-72f0a037bbf0", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (04:02): Well, and I think maybe to expand on what you're saying, it seems like today everyone assumes or believes that they need to be growing on social or they need to be executing on all the new platforms that are showing up or YouTube launches shorts, and then everyone's like, oh crap, we got to jump on this and we got to start to take advantage of these opportunities. And yeah, I'm really curious to dive in and understand what are the opportunities that certain brands or certain companies should go after. So I'm curious, where do we want to start? If you had to break down, if we had to simplify this idea into two really basic camps, your company needs to grow on social and there's the camp of maybe you don't need to grow on social at that very, very high level, how would you start to break apart people or companies into those two camps?", "token_estimate": 217, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:02", "end_timestamp": "04:02", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "151e0d3d-9fd4-4a8b-9b16-e82f23c4e5ee", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (05:07): So I always talk about as two goals with social. There's lead nurture, which is a fancy way of just being, I would describe it, your socials. Now, your website, your modern website, which is some brands that's maybe gyms, maybe like your local coffee shop doesn't need to have a million followers. Your local coffee shop just needs to look nice, be active. And when one friend shares it to another, they go, Ooh, that's cool. I'm going to go there. And maybe they have a first time discount or something on that Instagram that really incentivizes people to lead in. So that would be what I'd say, lead nurture. It's like your website. They have the same, you need to answer the same questions that anyone would if they came to your website, but people just want in this weird social format of story highlights and one line bio, all this stuff. (05:57): So that's one camp and that's more like upfront work, and that's maybe just kind of putting your pieces all together and then maybe one, two posts a week. You just want to be so you're active and you're not dead. So just like if you have reviews or whatever, you want to not be like, wait, no one's been there ever. It's like, is this still open? So that's one big camp. And the other camp I would say is the people that want to have the goal of growing and getting new people in the door because of social. So that means you show up in there, feed and they go, that's cool. I'm going to go to that place or buy that product. So that's what I'd say is that's lead generation.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:40): Is it too simple to break it down into service businesses versus product-based businesses?", "token_estimate": 417, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:07", "end_timestamp": "06:40", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "1354fe1f-a7aa-4d28-8540-4633d2643244", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (06:48): I would say so. I think there's a lot of layers to that. That's why I kind of go down. One of the first things we do with clients is audience. Who's your audience and what's the goals? And I think that's because this leads into that really nicely. I'm trying to figure out how much effort should you put, how much budget should you put towards this I, and yet it's really being realistic. Also, when I'm thinking about which bucket they fit in is are you reaching how localized is your target audience? Is your target audience us? Is your target audience, Washington Is your target audience, Vancouver? If the smaller it gets, the more likely your budget should be smaller, in my opinion, in terms of active posting from organic. There's other things I would suggest you do on social with that budget, but the broader you get, if you're an app that anyone can use around the world, you probably should be using. There's a good way you could use social, especially if you've got a bunch of content in that app that would do well in social. So that's kind of my, it's maybe less of a binary, but more of a spectrum that puts you towards one side or the other. And really it's just budget allocation I think is the main thing and some of the approaches you'll take.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:11): Interesting. Okay. When brands come to you and you're starting to talk about these high level goals and trying to understand the value of building the social, what are some of the things that you see pop up? Common themes around quantifying the value of social?", "token_estimate": 393, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:48", "end_timestamp": "08:11", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "e579e31b-2d23-4f79-8954-4177e6f5697a", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (08:29): Yep. The biggest question and arguably the, I'd say near in quotes, impossible answer my answer always when clients bring that up, and I'll just give guess that I think it's one, it's what do we track? You have to attract something. My KPI will almost never be revenue as an organic social. I would say that's, it's always going to be organic social is always going to be step one of a multi-step process that leads to revenue. So what we track on the social side is three things in the funnel. The top is awareness in terms of impressions. So how far are we getting out? Because impressions also, we're starting to quantify a little bit more in the cost it would take to pay for those impressions. Because I think that right now the average on Instagram, if you're going to pay for a thousand views, it's somewhere around the $8 mark according to few sources. (09:35): And maybe it's smaller depending on industry and a bunch, but broad average. So when you start taking that, there's some way you can bring some quantify, if we wanted to reach a million people, it would cost seven grand. Okay, how can we do that for cheaper? And that's probably organic and leveraging what the platforms want. So anyways, high level impressions and then we go to website, then we go to followers. So we want to see if we're reaching a bunch of people when they come to the profile and it's clear what we are and who we are, do they actually want to follow? Are they actually the right people? And if they are, how many of those people actually want to go to the website? And now is our website traffic changing because of our effort? If there's no, then I'm starting to be concerned that maybe you're getting followers, but they're still not the right audience member or you're right customers. So it's like you got to make sure that there's not a hole in the funnel of we get a bunch of people to be here, we get a bunch of followers, but they're all actually, we set up the profile so wrong that they're just here for climbing clips or they're just here for, I don't know, satisfying videos or something weird. They're not actually here for the product. So you got to make sure that that funnel, there's no holes in how you think about those three levels.", "token_estimate": 567, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "08:29", "end_timestamp": "08:29", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "84819dcd-1eca-425f-9be8-2786abd6a9d4", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:49): Can we pause there? Can I ask a follow up question? Yeah,\n\nSam Van Boxtel (10:51): Totally.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:52): Alright, so to talking about impressions to followers, to website traffic, I'm curious to focus on that first conversion impression to followers. What about a channel or a platform? We'll either take an impression and turn them into a follower or turn them away and send them the opposite direction.\n\nSam Van Boxtel (11:15): So many things here. So let's take it to the top. What gets you impressions? So then we'll say of those impressions, how do those get to follow? So impressions are all based on, you got to think of the exchange. This will go super high level here. The platform. The platform will give you more impressions when you give the platform what it wants in terms of keeping people on the platform, having them engage and show that they're satisfied with the content you're giving them super, super high level if you do that, and we can break that down further later on. What is optimized content that's\n\nCole Heilborn (11:48): Like taking advantage of reels or shorts or things that the algorithm is pushing that helps with your impression?", "token_estimate": 300, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "10:49", "end_timestamp": "11:48", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "86259717-ab10-4d98-9d8d-0275e2670ed7", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (11:55): I'd say yes, it's new features and it's, but almost everything goes back to still we've grown accounts based on just carousels or images. Still this year it's not real. It's because everyone's saved and shared and followed based on those posts because they were really in depth and they were like workout. They were workout summaries in one graphic and they were really well done and they were valuable. So those alone were able to drive growth just because again, they kept people on the platform and people liked them, engage them, share whatever that looks like to the people. So that's what the platform wants. If you give them that, they'll give you impressions, but now impressions are meaningless unless those convert, like we said down the funnel. And the main thing that determines if they'll become a follower is two things. It's like one is the content aligned with when they go to the profile, they're going to say, is it aligned with what they expect? So there's a few questions they're going to ask of I always as soon as possible. How do you get across what your brand is in one sentence? Usually on Instagram there's that bold line. That's what I tell all brands. Typically if your handle, so your at can be your brand name, your bold line at the top, I think it's called a name should be your one liner. I want to say brand is blank.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:16): Well, maybe it's like climbers CRA is social media Agency for climbing brands.", "token_estimate": 370, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:55", "end_timestamp": "13:16", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "5f532fcf-a78c-4bb3-b8d1-ecfdc2f92da3", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (13:22): Perfect. Exactly. Yep. So it's like the what do people categorize you as? So I try to get across that right away in the profile. So yeah, so when they're coming, they're like, did the content attract the right people that would actually want to follow the Climbers Crack agency in this case? So if I posted a TikTok dance to the Climbers Crack agency account, let's say my dancing skills are amazing and it gets a million views, those are not going to convert to followers and they shouldn't. I hope not because they're bad followers and they're not aligned with our customers down the funnel. So yeah. So I'd say alignment of content is the main thing in terms of what drives those followers. If you really want to be nerdy and look at the data, you want to look at the conversion rate of profile visits to followers. (14:11): This is how you can clue in on how is my content aligned and is my profile clear and giving to people a reason to follow. So a lot of profiles might just be a link or just might be one line. It's like, cool, you're an agency. So sometimes you need to give people context on why they would follow it, what's the content they're going to get? Or maybe they scroll down to the feed and it's all over the place. Disjointed. Is it a dance thing or is it an agency or is it climbing videos? Is it, what are you guys? So those are some of the factors. But on the conversion rate thing, so if you want to track profile visits and you want to go into followers, just if you guys want to write down some numbers are if you're under 10%, that should be your top priority. (15:00): Your profile, I want to just say sucks. People are coming or the content you're putting out is so far not aligned that they're going, wait what? So if it's under 10% of those profile visits to new followers, that's the ratio you want to come out. And if it's in the 20% range, you'd say average, it's not top priority. Maybe it could be better, 30% range. You're like, wow, good. Good job profile is great. Now you just need to reach more people with your content. Now it's like, okay, how do you turn up the dial of giving Instagram more of what they want? Can you post more? Can you make it a little more optimized? And if you're above 40%, you yeah, top tier, you have great profile, everyone loves you. The highest number I've seen is from one of my creator friends who's a climber. (15:53): Joseph Diaz climbs, I think it was somewhere around 80% profile visits, which is just, I didn't believe the number. When I saw it. I was looking at his analytics, I was like, wait. He was like, is my profile good? And I was like, let's see, let's do the math. And I was like, I don't believe the numbers. So that is possible. And that tells you, again, when we have some accounts and they start out under 10%, I'm going, Ooh, okay. So even if you had a great content piece that hit the right audience, they're not going to convert or with this profile that we're giving them, we need to improve that in terms of why people should follow what you guys do. And yet already we put together. So", "token_estimate": 777, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "13:22", "end_timestamp": "13:22", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "5cfcc084-d295-46d1-8ffa-c13ef153fd8c", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:34): Interesting. So let's say that listeners have that first piece figured out, they've got the compressions to followers dialed, they're all sitting at 50% and feeling really good about their conversion rates. Now to take it to the next step from followers to website traffic. How do you think about that?\n\nSam Van Boxtel (16:52): Yep. I would say that there's two main factors that will drive this, potentially a third, but the first thing I would think about is having a clear CTA. So everyone on Instagram, you have one link in the TikTok, most platforms you have one link. Having the last line of your bio have a clear CTA towards that link letting people know what it is. So on the one link that you have, having a clear CTA on the last line of the bio, letting people know what the link is, I think is huge. I always tell people I as a user, I don't like to click links. I don't know what they are. I think this is the classic email spam culture of don't touch that sketchy link. So I always try to be very clear, is it a shop link? Is it a new product, is it a free course, is it a email? Whatever it is, you should have a very clear call to action one thing, and you should be clear on what that is. (17:49): So that's the first thing. People need to know what the link in the bio is. Typically, if you can, I would try to not have it be the homepage. Sometimes it makes sense for it to be the homepage. If you don't have anything you're any freebie or anything you're running at the moment. But otherwise, I'd try to have it be something specific. And then the story highlights is typically the second place that you'll drive your other website traffic, which is, this goes back to the analogy of the account being a website, having your story highlights then would be to your tabs of your website. So these might be things like the different services you provide, the different products you provide. So like Friction Labs has their four main styles of chalk clearly shown so that you can click in and maybe you see, I think ours is pretty simple, it just has the link, but you could also click in and then show maybe a benefit or two of that product or what makes it different. (18:46): And then you have a link there to go to that straight to that page and you're not wasting any links. So I'm always trying to send people where they want. Again, going back to the website analogy, you don't want to have 10 million clicks to get to the place they want. So I like showing them the options in those story highlights. So maybe it's work with us, maybe it's our work. If you're more of a service-based thing. I'm trying to think of some other good examples. Could be testimonials and things like that. Trying to answer the questions that they have because again, they're trying to figure out who you are. Can they help me? How do I work with you? All the same questions that you should immediately answer on our website should be immediately answered on the Instagram bio. (19:33): Yeah, and I guess this is my one beef with Link Tree for the people with Link Trees. The link tree phase that went through is, I like having the story highlights more than that because it shows the options right there. They can see, do you have a YouTube channel that you want me to go to? Do you have a free course or a freebie that you can put those all there and you can have a visual experience. So they click into YouTube, you can show the top videos and they can just click into that video or you can show a playlist. You can just be more creative than a link tree, which is one, it's hard to drive people to that because it's a link tree. It's like, ooh, check out my free courses and my thing and my thing and my other thing. It's like that's not a very direct call to action at the bottom of your bio. And it just kind of adds that extra link friction of I have to go to the link tree to pick which link I want to watch versus just seeing the link tree options. Now, I also know as your brand, oh, you have free things, free courses. Oh, you do have a YouTube. I don't have to dig deep to find that it's there again, clear, just like your website or whatever. Oh, they do consulting. Oh, they do account management. I don't need you to dig to find that information. I want that to be clear.", "token_estimate": 1078, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "16:34", "end_timestamp": "16:52", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "028408da-fd9f-46dd-b964-bc1af97a3b39", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (20:43): Interesting. So those are all great examples for a service. We talked a lot about service-based businesses there. You mentioned some product. If you're a product-based business, some ideas there like highlighting your 10 best chucks, et cetera. What are some other ways that brands could use those story highlights to send people in a direction? Let's say if they're running a video series, would they be recommended to send people to that video series with those story highlights?", "token_estimate": 126, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:43", "end_timestamp": "20:43", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "1344074c-86d6-4b83-ac7c-3bc0b44d4004", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (21:13): Yeah, I'll actually pivot this slightly because I think at all times, an Instagram account, an Instagram account should have a few different one to three call to actions at all times because, and maybe this would've been a better place to start earlier was I think Instagram and all social breaks down into the point of it is get attention and have that attention learn about something you want them to learn about. It's the highest level. So if you get a bunch of attention and you don't have any clear what's your why, what do you want people to know about? Hopefully it's valuable and adds value to them because that's what you need to have a business. But yeah, so that would go back to whatever your CTAs are, your CTAs are, should show up in the story highlights should show up in the bio. So I think I would almost push back to whoever's saying, what do I put there? (22:08): It's like, what do you want? Do you want people to know about your new product? Do you want them to know about your, I don't know, infinite amount of things, but how do you display that and how do you showcase that? Is it new products? Is it this feature that no one knows about that you wish people would know about? Is it this FAQ? Is it infinite amounts of things? But I would push back and say, that's a good thing though. Going back to wasting money, that is probably the number one way. It's just like we just want to get a bunch of views. It's like, okay, but what is the clear? Also, if you can boil those down to one sentence, okay, it's the sentence for maybe this offer or this holiday sale right now in Friction Labs, it's like we want to let people know for the next month, or as long as the sale is going on, this is the holiday sale, you're not going to get this. Again, here's our one sentence. How do we get that in captions? How do we get that in posts? How do we get that in the profile? So\n\nCole Heilborn (23:08): What are some call to actions that work really well and what are some that you've seen that maybe don't? And I realize it's probably very audience specific, but are there some call to actions that you should just avoid on social altogether?", "token_estimate": 546, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:13", "end_timestamp": "23:08", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "e9dc83f8-1e6e-4a05-94f0-219e4d26ce9b", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (23:25): Yeah, that's a good question. I would say anything that, you always got to be careful when you're asking people to go off platform or change mindsets. So anything that, that's why it's always hard to, when people put in an Instagram post, it's like, Hey, new YouTube video. It's like, cool. But I'm looking through Instagram feed right now. I'm not here to watch YouTube videos. The notorious example is in Instagram stories. So yeah, the worst example I would say is on Instagram stories. When people make a link sticker and they'll say, new video or go buy my product or something. And it's like I'm in a stories feed. I am doing this one thing. So the quick hack or wait, best practice we found on there is what we'll call the DM for DM for link. So it's like doing a poll where you say, do you want to watch our new film or do you want us to send you the link? (24:28): Do you want this discount? Is the question of the poll and it's yes or no. So then if people say yes, you can just copy paste, send them the link. You can add a little personalization if you want to make it even more over the top one, you might start a conversation out of that, which could lead to a sale or whatever and just make you a little bit more personal. But also what happens is you get that link not in the stories experience, but now you get this link in the dm, which is when they want. They can come back to it at any time. And it's a much different mindset than being in the social stories of like, ah, swipe, swipe, swipe, swipe, swipe. Same with the feed. How can you get people into their dms? Or how do you get, this is why email is so good. Email is a much better mindset than you are on social. You got to think, this is one of the challenges is you might have to tell people that CTA over and over, because they're in that mindset of lots of things are hitting them. So CTAs, yeah, it's always hard to get 'em off platform or get them away from where they're at. And trying to think about how do you ask for, how do you make it as frictionless as possible in terms of where they have to go to get that offer.", "token_estimate": 532, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "23:25", "end_timestamp": "23:25", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "a6aa65e2-3b24-4bf6-ad18-c0b3b6f38052", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (25:46): Well, it either sounds like the offer to take them off the platform needs to be so good that they have to jump on it now, or you need to give them way to quickly come back to that offsite experience and find it and then follow through with it at a later point where maybe it's more convenient time.\n\nSam Van Boxtel (26:06): And I don't think the CTAs need to be, again, when I was going back to every branch should have one to three. These don't need to be perfectly crafted. Don't be overwhelmed by this. This is just need to be simply aligned on, we want email subscribers. Cool, and this month we're going to do a giveaway so everyone who signs up is entered to win. Okay, we need to let people know that we're doing this giveaway to collect email addresses. That's just one of your three things. Oh, we have a sale or we don't have any sale. We just want people to buy this product. Just be clear. That's the one we're going to be talking about this month. So it's like don't let this idea of call to action overwhelm you too much, but it's like having a clear goal of where you want the traffic to go, because collecting attention. (26:52): And then it's like, where do you want to send people? If you don't have clear on that, you'll go months with not sending people anywhere. And they'll just be like, cool. I mean, I saw something cool. I think I've seen that brand before, but I have no idea what they do or what they want me to know about. So that's when, again, you get into that territory of maybe you're getting a bunch of impressions, maybe you're getting a bunch of followers, but you have a big hole. Trying to figure out that last step of where you want people to actually show up. Where do you want to see the conversion? Because again, sometimes it's not revenue, it's maybe email list or it's showing up in person or going to a store or something like that.", "token_estimate": 476, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "25:46", "end_timestamp": "26:06", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "ad4ed1e6-6ea1-409f-9ef1-4448ef4fdcd5", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (27:34): So, all right. I'm curious to transition a little bit and start to talk about platforms and which platforms brands should be considering. Obviously platforms rise and fall, there's new things that pop up and then they disappear, and then there's some that have lasted for quite a while. How do you begin to decipher what platforms a brand or company should be focusing on?\n\nSam Van Boxtel (27:57): So typically I would say the short answer is all roads lead back to Instagram. But the longer answer, most roads lead back to Instagram. The longer answer is, I guess just working through Facebook I think is where you'll see a lot of brands. Right now, Facebook, the only value I really see for most brands is being from an organic point of view, all this is organic. Facebook paid is still alive and well, it's expensive, but it's alive and well. So the only advantage I might see to post organically on Facebook would to be active so that if you were running paid traffic, when they check out your profile, just like I've talked about earlier on Instagram, when they check it out, you're active and you tick all the boxes of, okay, they're alive, they answer all my questions, et cetera. But outside, if you're not doing any paids, I don't really see much value on Facebook. (28:50): The reach, organic reach is just really low. And that on the high level, high level, why do platforms have bad organic reach versus good organic reach? I don't love when people are the algorithms pushing this or that. It's like that's really not the thing too much. It's all supply and demand, basic economics, classic. It's all how much content is there and how many people are wanting more content viewers to content. So when reels opens up two years ago, there's people that were like, what is this new thing? And then there was not that much content. So if you posted content, it did well. Same with TikTok. Now if you post from TikTok, it's so much harder because everyone's flocked to creating content and to view there that now it's closer to equilibrium and Facebook to bring it back to Facebook. Facebook has so much content that right now it's just between the ads and all the things they want to show you, there's just not, your content has to be so good to be shown. (29:55): So Facebook, I kind of moved through pretty quickly. YouTube is the next one. YouTube I think is the most stable, most long-term platform is that it's almost not a social platform. I feel like it's on its own tier of, it's a search engine that's social as opposed to Instagram, Facebook, TikTok, these are all purely social and you're just there for the quick content. These will rise and fall in perpetuity. There'll be a new one in five years from now for sure. I just know it. I dunno what it'll be, but YouTube I think sits on its own, but the barrier to entry is so high. And I think to be honest, that goes back to the equilibrium thing. It's so hard for there to be that much content compared to the viewers because no one can just, I mean, without shorts, if you take out shorts, it takes work to make a five minute, 10 minute, 12 minute video, and the amount of brands or whatever you see theirs so much lower. Because I tell brands, you got to be somewhere in the a hundred K in the first year investment to really make this happen. By the time you get the content who's filming, editing the idea so hard to do it. Right. Yeah.", "token_estimate": 858, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "27:34", "end_timestamp": "27:57", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "46f7190e-004a-4747-be09-42cc6b2da37e", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (31:09): Well that depends significantly on the type of content you're creating too.\n\nSam Van Boxtel (31:13): For sure. Yeah. Yeah. So YouTube is the highest investment. I think it's the highest long-term reward because I think it kind of transcends the social game, so to speak. And so then I moved to Instagram is where most roads lead back to for starting. I'll talk about TikTok right after that. Tiktoks usually the upgrade, or is I say Instagram's kind of the warmup to TikTok because tiktoks, I think even harder on the short form content side. So Instagram, the reason I like it so much is just because you can have so much under one roof in terms of stories, feed posts and reels. Now you have three different ways to connect with people. And some people can use things like the shop and dms as well. So it's like four main ways you can connect with people. And the struggle with a place like TikTok is you have one way to connect with people and it only likes the best content. (32:19): So if your content's kind of lame or salesy or you want to let people know about that holiday sale and your post isn't amazing, no one's going to see it. If you're on Instagram, you could put it in a story. You could DM your a hundred biggest customers or whatever and make sure that they see it and say, Hey, here's the discount. I dunno if you saw this, you could show it, put in the feed and you can make a reel about it. And now I'm pretty confident that a lot more people are going to see that regardless of if it's a flashy, quick, valuable, funny, real or short form piece of content that might do well on TikTok. So that's the why right now on Instagram, you can grow, you have the profile and you have the capabilities of multiple ways to reach those people that I just no other platform has. (33:12): TikTok has the potentially slightly more reach at the moment, and you can grow, but again, I'm always worried that you'll end up with a hundred thousand followers and it'll be even harder to direct people to those CTAs. Like that funnel we talked about. It's really hard, and I'm really unsure still. We're testing with a few brands right now. We're trying to see that bottom piece of the funnel. How does that do on TikTok? I don't know. I'm not going to claim I have the answer, but that's my biggest question. And I know for more brands than most brands, it's hard to get that final piece from followers to, oh, I'm actually the person that wants this thing and I'm going to go buy this thing. There's some unique cases where your product is so interesting that your product will do well in content and then people can go straight by that thing. I know those brands blow up on TikTok. They have the reach, they have the followers, and it's connected to, I'm", "token_estimate": 696, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "31:09", "end_timestamp": "31:13", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "189e3bca-883d-4725-b661-781f1a02a4cd", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (34:10): Thinking about someone like Liquid Death, they stick out as a brand who hits that happy medium.\n\nSam Van Boxtel (34:16): So it's definitely can be done. I don't, but in our experience, I'm still most unsure of that. That's why I'm always saying on Instagram, you still can grow and you can do more people. You can really build a community and interact with people way more creatively than any other platform. So it's kind of a no competition for me at the moment. But again, like I said, I think all these social platforms that aren't YouTube are going to just cycle in and out. So it's really being on top of what is the thing right now? How do you become good at it? How do you look at data and always improve? So one of the biggest things for any of you guys out there that's running an account every week, how do you look at what's going well and make any kind of pivot every single week? (35:05): It sounds like a lot, but really in social it's not. So yeah, if you do that, that's when you'll be ahead of, Ooh, maybe we should try TikTok. Ooh, let's test there every week. Okay, maybe oh, doesn't make sense yet, since yet we did two months. We got no return from it, no growth. We missed our content. Maybe we'll come back in six months. Oh, now our audience is here. This content did well and now you're in it and now you're ready when Instagram dies on the next platform. So again, if you're not pivoting, you won't make it on organic. If you are pivoting and you're actually doing well, I kind of think you'll be fine no matter what. Because like we said, with the platforms somewhere, we'll have the viewers and not enough content. So you just go there and you make better content than everyone else and you'll get that organic attention. So it's just being ahead of it and always testing and pivoting.", "token_estimate": 459, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "34:10", "end_timestamp": "34:16", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "8b8f08c0-b6ab-42cd-8f7d-ea3e33332ad4", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (36:01): So talk to me a little bit about the timing of jumping on a platform, because we would assume that it's best to jump on a platform from the very beginning. So you take advantage of all the newness, and as you're talking about that supply and demand idea, obviously the Instagram example is one that's commonly used. You're like, shoot, I should have jumped on that when it first came out. But that's not always true, and I'm curious what your logic is behind that.", "token_estimate": 122, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:01", "end_timestamp": "36:01", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "1b245800-b2a4-4e06-ae3a-82501cf4705c", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (36:32): Yeah, this is a debate Tom and I have all the time. We've had for probably 12 to 16 months about TikTok, to be honest. Every couple months we're like, is it time? Is there enough climbers there? Because again, almost all of our brands are climbing in the climbing industry. So we're trying to figure out, are climbers there? Is it actually worth putting in the effort? And yeah, no, we keep circling back all around of do we see pros? Are we seeing brands move there? So yeah, some of the things, the main thing I want to look at is are people in our industry using it? Because again, we might get the impressions, but are we going to get the right followers and are they going to convert? And yeah, until recently, we spent no budget, no time, I guess back in 2021 when we first had this discussion and Tom said, let's try it. (37:23): Let's see if we can do it. We in six months went to 60 K on e climbers, Greg, and then we pulled the plug and we said, nothing's happening. Nothing in the bottom half is happening at all. We moved away, we have 60,000 followers, totally inactive. I was like, I don't know about this thing. I dunno if this is going to last. I don't know how brands will use this. I think it's been somewhere close to 12 months since then that point. It's now when we're testing with two or three brands right now, and they've just gotten to that point where they have a couple thousand followers and we're trying to figure out, cool, we have that, but what's the conversion and is this worth long-term budget or is this just like a test and cool, we tested it, let's check back in 12 months. (38:06): So the test is still out. So I mean the climbing industry, I'm not sure every industry is slightly different, which is funny because tiktoks not the new thing anymore in 2020, it was the new thing. It's two years past that and climbers still aren't hugely there. If you look at the amount of climbers over 20 to 50,000 followers, there's maybe a handful, I suppose on Instagram. I dunno, thousands that are over that mark. So it's like the penetration just really isn't there in that industry. So I would be looking at whatever industry you're in, how many counts have hit that scale? Are they actually, can you see that they're continuing putting budget behind it or is this on the side for them? You got to also keep in mind if it's a Red Bull, oh, and they're putting a little bit of effort, it's like, okay, well let's throw away money. (39:01): They're not. But if you see people go in hard and really take advantage and growing, that's when I'd be, when I would say, okay, it's time. So going back to your point of how early do you go, I'm the mindset of I want to see someone else crushing it a little bit before I do. Yeah, there's always, again, goes back to spectrum. The earlier you are, the better your chance you have, but also the more likely you are to waste your money because the platform dies, it's TikTok, it gets banned in the country or whatever. All these things that people are saying, I don't think that'll happen, but you just don't know what's going to happen until a platform hits that scale, especially in your industry. So was that helpful?", "token_estimate": 804, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "36:32", "end_timestamp": "36:32", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "903c8857-27a1-4ef0-9b0b-315640281d42", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (39:43): Definitely, definitely. So we're on the topic of platforms. What platforms are you keeping an eye on for future things that might become the next Instagram? Is there anything that's out there that could be\n\nSam Van Boxtel (39:58): Nothing? I've heard everything. My attention's, 99% of my attention's going to YouTube, Instagram and TikTok. I think that's kind of the combo for most brands and most brands. I also like to say once you get to the point where you're posting, let's say every day on Instagram, you're seeing solid growth compared to your industry average. That's also another thing I like to use is it's hard to say what's good. So I always like to say, so we did research on the top 150 brands and the top 150 creators in climbing and said, how much are they growing every month? Because you can see that data in social blade.com. I love that. All the public data. So you can actually see what is the industry average of growth and how do you stack against that? So let's say you're posting consistently, you have a good system for creating and posting content. (40:51): Say you're double, triple, quadruple the industry average. You probably should be, just to give you guys context, the climbing industry is like 0.75 growth a month in terms of followers. So every month less than 1% is what brands are growing. All of our clients as a whole, I just looked last month and we're at like 13% on average. So most times there's probably larger room to beat that industry average. But let's say you got a good system, that's when I'd say maybe it's time to go to TikTok as well. I have the short form content. I'm doing reels, our audience likes it, our target audience likes this. Cool. Now maybe we can upgrade and bring in another platform, but I always like to say master the most important one right now. And then once you get to the point where it feels pretty good, got a good growth rate, which to me is the health of the account. If you're growing, that means you're doing well, you're seeing a good conversion. That's when I'd potentially add another platform in which right now I would think is TikTok most times", "token_estimate": 540, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "39:43", "end_timestamp": "39:58", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "ae71d3d9-e526-4b4e-ad3e-88832d6030be", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (41:53): Who is YouTube for. We talked a little bit about how difficult it is and how much of a long game YouTube is. When is it worth the investment?\n\nSam Van Boxtel (42:03): I think I'm most excited about it, and the only two times I bring it up is when you are already doing some kind of long form content creation. Let's say things like a podcast or we're working with Crux Academy, which is doing basically the masterclass for the outdoors. So they're filming long form courses. I said, Hey, you have the best athletes for several days filming. We should do a YouTube channel that's maybe some of the course content and maybe some original content that's made for YouTube. Again, feels like a pretty natural, it's not going to be as much of that investment. It's going to still be a big investment, but it's more adjacent to what they're doing and their goals as a business. So I'm always excited when there's an adjacent, when you're already creating some kind of long form content, because I think the barrier is so long, so big to really get started any kind of this content. But if you're already doing it in some capacity, that's when I'd say, how can you do it for YouTube? Or how can you add a YouTube portion to it? That's when I think YouTube makes sense. I'm sure there's more setups than that, but in my experience, that's when I'm like, yes, this can work. (43:18): Yeah, I was going to say, and then what's great about YouTube is at some point once you get it going, you can be paid AdSense and you can hopefully, if you could break even on your marketing engine who's getting millions of views, maybe not millions of views, even hundreds of thousands of views in right of the people, and you're getting break even or even making money. I mean, that's a good system to have. Sign me up for that. So", "token_estimate": 458, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "41:53", "end_timestamp": "42:03", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "7dfab48a-ea14-4162-b223-e586c26cb210", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Cole Heilborn (43:41): Yeah, I'm curious to hear your thoughts on this. So frequently it happens in our world where a client will come to us and they talk about YouTube and they talk about we want to create stories. Basically they want to duplicate Yeti or Patagonia, and they want to try to replicate that for themselves, which is great. We love that type of content and we love getting to tell those stories. I think one piece though, why it often doesn't work and why we end up pivoting away from that when we work with clients is because their audience isn't trained for that type of content or they're not ready for that type of content. How do you train an audience to expect, in this case, a Patagonia example, how do you train an audience to expect that type of content?\n\nSam Van Boxtel (44:32): I don't know. That's a good question. I would like, yeah, I would love to dig in this because curious on your side, what my brain instantly went to. If the brand isn't something that has a broad target audience in terms of who use it, where they use it, it goes kind of over to the organic side of like, should you really spend that much effort or should you be more localized in paid ads, local marketing, whatever that is. I guess I'm curious from what you've seen, is that, do you think that's a part of it, or I don't know. I'm going to ask you flip it on you. What do you think?\n\nCole Heilborn (45:08): I mean, there's, the reason I asked you the question is I don't know the answer.\n\nSam Van Boxtel (45:12): What do you think? I don't know the examples. I haven't quite been in that situation yet.", "token_estimate": 402, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "43:41", "end_timestamp": "45:12", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "a946d330-278a-40f7-8bef-3715e825da9a", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Cole Heilborn (45:18): So I guess two examples. Recently we worked on a feature documentary called Bike Town. It was in collaboration with Freeh Hub and a bunch of other bike sponsors to bring the project to life. And it's a 45 minute film about how mountain bike communities have formed unlikely partnerships with land managers to develop trail communities and thriving trail communities. And we did a whole distribution, a live distribution around the country. It's now on Free Hub magazine's, YouTube channel, and it's doing really well. I think Free Hub has done an excellent job of building a platform though, where people, it's a community platform. People come to the platform because they want to learn something, they want to hear a story. And I think for Free Hub, their background, they started as a magazine telling stories about mountain bikers and communities. And so it makes a lot of sense that the YouTube channel is just an extension of their core product, which is their magazine. (46:19): So I think in that instance, that's why our film has seen success and continues to grow in views. I think situations where we have produced projects for companies, I'm thinking of, we've produced some brand films and brand films are questionable these days if they're actually helpful or useful. And I think it's very case by case dependent, but I think the biggest thing is in the times where it hasn't worked or there hasn't been a bunch of, let's say, engagement behind the brand piece is when the company doesn't really know who their audience is or what their relationship with their audience is. And as we've done those projects in the past, now we're at a point where we're like, you need to have that figured out before we're even going to start talking about a story or start talking about making a film. Because I mean, if it's not going to land and if it's not useful, then it's not worth our time or your time to invest in this. And so I feel like that's the biggest thing is a disconnect between understanding the relationship between the brand and their audience.", "token_estimate": 522, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "45:18", "end_timestamp": "45:18", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "7d104cba-016e-40d9-8bd7-164b44be90e4", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (47:29): And maybe once you figure out the audience, my brand also went to this because thinking about doing this on the climbers, Craig YouTube channel is having some of those branded films, I know they're being made, but instead of being distributed on some small brands 2000 subscriber channel, it's like pairing with a platform that you know can get distribution on that aligns with that audience. I think that is kind of where my brain goes if you know, don't have a following, I guess building the, I'm always thinking about distribution, but thinking about that before you get too far down the process of making the film is like, where's this going? Can we partner with some platform like you said, that is aligned in the audience and would feel natural for that story to show up? And again, in theory, you're giving them a good story and something they'll like if that audience is that target audience. And that's where I would see it doing well versus you just saying out of the blue, it's like, alright, this is first video, we're going to be Yeti and we're this local coffee shop. And it's like, oh, I mean, that's going to be a grind maybe you pair up with, yeah. So that's just where I'd be, I would think about distribution audience, like you said. (48:41): But yeah, that's a good one. Yeah, branded films always. I would love to pick your brain more on that, but we won't go into that because I'm curious about those.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:50): Let's talk a little bit about performance versus quality in terms of overall approach. You mentioned this earlier, and I wanted to circle back to it. Do you focus, I guess, how do you think about that? Is it better to do super quality but fewer posts or just tons of posts that are", "token_estimate": 439, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "47:29", "end_timestamp": "48:50", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "2d210151-95df-4892-9885-0ba9070560e2", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (49:10): Yeah, so I guess I would define the terms here. Typically I say success, platform success and quality. Quality is I want to make a nice film, I want to put out a nice post I want to put out, it's all subjective. It is what quality. Quality to everyone is different. So that's kind of one end of, I also sometimes call it brand and success. If you want to talk about your brand and tell your exact brand messaging and you don't have any wiggle room and it needs to be a horizontal image, actually it needs to be a horizontal six images, and that's my brand, and we write long form, you could do that. But on the spectrum of brand slash quality to success, success is are you reaching the people? Going back to the funnel, all we've talked about so far has really been success impressions to followers, to website traffic. (50:05): So in that spectrum between brand and success platform success, it'll be a spectrum where maybe you'll want to be closer towards the brand side and you'll know that this post won't perform, but you know that it's going to be more clear. It's going to get across your brand. My brand wouldn't post a TikTok dance video maybe I doubt it's, it's not probably what's most successful, but it might be, or let's say in a climate example, it's always posting big Dinos the longest, Dino possible, the most shocking cool thing. Yeah, that might be platform successful, but it might not be at all connected to your brand if you're a climbing coffee brand or something. I don't know. So it's a spectrum of where you want to be. And when I build a strategy out, I'm always trying to balance each post and the strategy as a whole on, is this what I guess goes back to goals? (51:02): Do they want to be a big follower growth or big awareness, big impressions is a goal, or do they want to get their messaging across or they want to be very clear on who they are, who they aren't? Again, sometimes you can do both, but I typically think of it as spectrum of how successful do we want this to be? And once you start learning what does well, and again, like we said, when we're posting a hundred posts a week, we have a pretty decent sense on what will do success. And then we have the brand side to be like, what do they want or what do they want to get across? So I like to say that they're very distinctly different things, especially on YouTube. Especially on Instagram, you can put out something, you're like, this is amazing, why didn't people see this? I'm like, yes, it is amazing, but it's not made for, it's not going to too successful on the platform. So I just bringing that up early with brands and creators is big because it's their brand, it's their film they made. And I'm like, yes, but you got to think about the title before posting the video because otherwise why am I going to click on this? So yeah, success versus quality.", "token_estimate": 721, "speakers": ["Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "49:10", "end_timestamp": "49:10", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "5884c133-a17d-4805-98ce-c37c75fade6c", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Cole Heilborn (52:12): Another topic that I want to just brief over is when to outsource and when to manage all this. How do you think through that and basically when should someone reach out to an agency like yourself?\n\nSam Van Boxtel (52:26): Yeah, I feel like it's typically when, so going back to those two goals of lead nurture, where we started the call lead, nurture, lead generation. If anyone comes and it's maybe a gym or someone that's a local place and they should be in the lead nurture category, you've decided listen to the hour chat and you're like, I think we shouldn't go really hard and make a bunch of content. I think we should just look nice and have a nice profile that's active. If you fit in that category, that's when potentially you want, maybe it's maybe nice to get a consulting call or just some consulting input to help you just speed up that time to getting everything put into place. But that's when you can really handle that yourself. If you can have a staff member, whoever you are, someone just doing that one or two posts, again, it's about activity. (53:14): It's not about like, whoa, this was optimized for, this is blowing up. But when you move into the other category of lead generation one, that's when you just need to not have a staff member do it. Not just have your sister or your nephew or whatever. That's when you need someone who knows what they're doing. Because going like that is when you waste money is because brands just put out all the things we talked about today. Your niece or nephew's not going to probably have clear CTAs, they're not going to have a high level goal. They're not going to probably talk to you about the audience. They're going to just post what they, and they might be successful, they might post things that are, but they're going to probably be so unconnected to the brand that it could be a total waste. So that's when I would say really look at a freelancer or an agency, someone who has done it in your industry also. (54:06): I think that's important. I really don't think it translates insanely well across. If I did a skincare, social media, I think I would suck, to be honest. I would know the right questions to ask, but you wouldn't be able to just instantly have that plug and play. Here's the questions, here's the role, the high level principles of the platform. So I would look for a freelancer agency in your industry that's done stuff before. So I think that's the biggest thing. And I've heard with people sometimes before this is going to shoot ourselves in the foot of here, but a little bit. But the best way to really go about it is to have someone internal who's working with the agency, who's picking up on their processes systems and the way they do things. And hopefully they're trained up in six months a year and they're like, oh, they always do this. The guy I work with, he always does this. He asks this question, I take notes, I'm a big learner. And then potentially they could head up your social a year from now. I think we might lose some clients from that, but that's the best way. If I was running it run with the agency, but pay attention, ask the right questions, actually learn from them. (55:19): Again, you have the capacity most times. The thing is you just want to not think about it, which is fair as a business owner, you don't want to think about organic social, like so many other things to think about. But yeah, if you can have the foresight, that's the optimal route is bring in the expert, but for a limited amount of time with the right plan to get rid of them. Get rid of them once you have the knowledge. So yeah.", "token_estimate": 913, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "52:12", "end_timestamp": "52:26", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "ed1cb8f7-40be-4a64-bb18-e69e8c820f06", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 26, "content": "Cole Heilborn (55:42): Interesting. Makes sense. Yeah, find someone, I mean, it's true for all cases. Find someone who understands your industry and specializes in what you do so that you don't have to onboard them and help them understand your world. They get it.\n\nSam Van Boxtel (55:58): Yeah. The copy thing is so hard too, especially with organic social messaging is a decent amount of the whole piece of the pie, and if you have to teach them what a dino is or what any of these key terms in your industry, it infinitely becomes more work for you. When we talk to clients, I try to say, all we need is about an hour of your time every month, and that's because we know so much about the industry. If you can get us X, Y, and Z, we can do the rest. We can coordinate with athletes, we can find the content, we can cut the content. You just need to show up and say, is this content good? Where are we going as a brand? What goals do we have? Just so we can stay aligned and not be too far off the rails, but otherwise it'll be a bunch of your time. It'll say, Hey, can I have this content? Also, how do you say this in your industry also, is this right? And it's like, no, we would never say that. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (56:53): Well, Sam, I've really enjoyed this conversation. I feel like it's been a deep dive, but I know we could go even deeper if we had the time. I think what I've taken away from this conversation is, I mean everything, it all comes back to your goals, but we've covered impressions, call to actions, platforms. I think one big thing that we haven't talked about, we've talked all this has been under the umbrella of organic content and we haven't talked about paid content, so maybe we'll have to do a second episode and follow up with that umbrella.", "token_estimate": 445, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Sam Van Boxtel"], "start_timestamp": "55:42", "end_timestamp": "56:53", "podcast_index": 131, "episode_name": "EP: 131  How Brands are Wasting Money on Social media | Sam Van Boxtel | Climbers Crag", "slug": "ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-131-how-brands-are-wasting-money-on-social-media-sam-van-boxtel-climbers-crag", "guest_name": "Sam Van Boxtel", "guest_title": "Founder at Climbers Crag", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Sam discuss the most common mistakes outdoor brands make on social media, the value of social in the outdoor space, why social media is important for brand building, what metrics to track, and much, much more!", "podcast_length": "59:57"}, {"id": "da24399e-10b9-4062-8f35-556bbf6af0f7", "episode_id": "8d7ea5a3-6f4f-441d-a62e-624df90af9cb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a6", "chunk_index": 27, "content": "Sam Van Boxtel (57:24): Oh man, that'd be great. We're really working on paid an email right now trying to expand over the next few months and be good at that because organic is where we've really started and what we're good at and we're trying to figure out how to be just as good at paid and email. Because like I said, I think early on organic's usually step one of two or three in the customer journey or in the sales process. So us being able to help clients on steps two and three or whatever they may be, or just step two, I think just helps us add even more value. So\n\nCole Heilborn (57:57): Yeah. Sam, is there anything that you want to leave our audience with? Any last thoughts or anything we didn't cover?\n\nSam Van Boxtel (58:04): I mean we didn't cover so much, so shoot me an email, Sam at climbers Craig, I'll answer anything I can. We also do free consulting calls if you just want to pick my brain on something. We're doing that for now. I don't know how many emails before I shut that down and say, I don't have the time to do this, but yeah, send me an email. Say I'm at climbers Craig, we can do a quick chat. I can hear about what you guys are doing. I probably won't pitch you on something unless I think it really adds value. I'm always about the organic next step, so yeah, it's not going to be me coming in with an agenda on you. So yeah, send me an email. Where can folks go to find you or the YouTube channel or the agency. Yeah, so I hope if you can just search climbers Craig anywhere, you should be pretty clear. I like to think that we're okay at our jobs and letting you know where everything is. So yeah, if you search us, we're on YouTube climbers crag. The only thing that's kind of weird is on Instagram, we're a climbers crag agency. We have a second account there that shows testimonials, case studies, who we've worked with. But yeah, we also have a main page on Instagram as well. Climbers do crag, so I would be sad if you can't find what you want to find through Google, but yeah, that's my, yeah.", "token_estima